export default {"u1": "# Session map ::: {.keyidea title=\"Outcome of this session\"} After this session you can: 1. state the difference between a **discriminative** model $p(y\\mid x)$ and a **generative** model $p(x)$ or $p(x,y)$, and say what each can and cannot do; 2. write the training objective of an autoregressive model, an autoencoder, a VAE, a GAN and a diffusion model in one line each; 3. derive the VAE evidence lower bound (ELBO) and the GAN optimal discriminator, and say what a reparameterisation is for; 4. build and run a character-level text generator, and read its perplexity on training and held-out text; 5. explain hallucination, bias, privacy and copyright risk as consequences of *what generative models are trained to do*, with a measured example. ::: - **Prerequisites:** Bayes' rule, expectation, the logarithm; gradient descent; the idea of a neural-network layer. Session 001 (Unit 3) defined perplexity and next-token prediction, which are used here; session 002 (Unit 2) built the Transformer, which is only *named* here. - **Difficulty ladder used:** easy \u2192 medium \u2192 medium-hard \u2192 hard (Sections 3.1 to 3.4); the hardest tier is posed as open questions in Section 3.5. - **Deliverables:** this document (.tex, .pdf, .docx), 5 figures, the module `codes/genmodels/genmodels_np/` (classifiers, a character $n$-gram model, an MLP with hand-written backpropagation, an autoencoder, a VAE and a GAN; 7 tests including finite-difference gradient checks), and the *Text generator (n-gram)* and *Generative classifier* widgets in the LLM Studio playground. - **Source anchors:** Kingma and Welling 2013, arXiv:1312.6114 (VAE: Section 2.3 for the bound and Eq. (1), Section 2.4 for the reparameterisation trick); Goodfellow et al. 2014, arXiv:1406.2661 (GAN: Proposition 1 for the optimal discriminator, Theorem 1 for the $-\\log4$ minimum and Eq. (5) for the Jensen-Shannon form); Ho et al. 2020, arXiv:2006.11239 (diffusion: $T=1000$ steps and variances rising linearly from $10^{-4}$ to $0.02$, Section 4). All three were checked against arXiv and the quoted statements were read in the PDFs. # 1. Terms: the term tree | Term | Symbol | One-line meaning | Where it appears | New / known | |:-----|:------:|:-----------------|:-----------------|:-----------:| | discriminative model | $p(y\\mid x)$ | predicts a label from an input | 3.1 | new | | generative model | $p(x)$ | models the data itself, so it can create new data | 3.1 | new | | likelihood | $p_\\theta(x)$ | the probability the model assigns to the observed data | 3.2 | new | | autoregressive model | | generates one piece at a time, each conditioned on the earlier pieces | 3.2 | new | | $n$-gram model | | autoregressive model whose context is the last $n-1$ symbols | 3.2 | new | | autoencoder | | encoder squeezes the input to a short code, decoder rebuilds it | 3.2 | new | | latent variable | $z$ | a hidden, lower-dimensional description of the data | 3.2 | new | | variational autoencoder (VAE) | | autoencoder whose code is a distribution, trained on the ELBO | 3.3 | new | | ELBO | $\\mathcal{L}$ | evidence lower bound: reconstruction minus KL | 3.3 | new | | KL divergence | $D_{KL}$ | how different one distribution is from another (not symmetric) | 3.3 | new | | reparameterisation | | write $z=\\mu+\\sigma\\epsilon$ so gradients pass through sampling | 3.3 | new | | generative adversarial network (GAN) | | generator versus discriminator in a two-player game | 3.3 | new | | mode collapse | | the generator covers only part of the data distribution | 3.3 | new | | diffusion model | | learn to remove noise that was added step by step | 3.3 | new | | overfitting / memorisation | | reproducing training data instead of its pattern | 3.4 | new | Terms *used but not taught here*: attention and the Transformer block (Unit 2, session 002); tokenization and decoding (Unit 3, session 001); the full diffusion derivation (Unit 7). # 2. Remember ::: {.definition title=\"Discriminative versus generative\"} A **discriminative** model learns $p(y\\mid x)$: given a photo, is it a cat or a dog? A **generative** model learns the distribution of the data itself, $p(x)$ (or $p(x,y)=p(x\\mid y)\\,p(y)$): what does a cat photo look like, and can I make a new one? A generative classifier can still classify, by Bayes' rule $$ p(y\\mid x)=\\frac{p(x\\mid y)\\,p(y)}{\\sum_{y'}p(x\\mid y')\\,p(y')}. $$ {#eq:bayes} ::: ::: {.definition title=\"The six model families in one line each\"} | family | what it learns | training objective | |:--|:--|:--| | autoregressive | $p(x)=\\prod_i p(x_i\\mid x_{<i})$ | maximise the likelihood (minimise cross-entropy) | | autoencoder | a short code $z$ and a decoder | minimise reconstruction error $\\lVert x-\\hat x\\rVert^2$ | | VAE | a distribution over codes $q(z\\mid x)$ and a decoder $p(x\\mid z)$ | maximise the ELBO | | GAN | a generator that fools a discriminator | a minimax game | | diffusion | to predict and remove noise | regress the added noise at every noise level | | Transformer | an *architecture* (attention blocks), not a family | whatever objective it is trained with | ::: **Memory hooks.** - *Discriminative = judge, generative = forger.* The judge only needs to tell real from fake; the forger must know enough to make something that passes. - *Autoencoder = a suitcase.* Pack the data into a small suitcase (the code) and unpack it again; whatever does not fit is lost. - *VAE = a suitcase with a tolerance.* The code is allowed to be fuzzy, which makes the space *between* codes meaningful, so you can sample new ones. - *GAN = counterfeiter and detective; diffusion = restoring a painting that was gradually buried in noise.* **Recall quiz** (answers in Section 10). 1. Which of $p(y\\mid x)$ and $p(x)$ lets you *create* new data? 2. What does an autoregressive model use as its context when it predicts the next symbol? 3. Write the two terms of the VAE loss. 4. What does the optimal GAN discriminator output when the generator is perfect? 5. Name two ways a generative model can leak or misuse its training data. # 3. Understand: the derivation ## 3.1 Tier 1 (easy): intuition and a picture Most machine learning you have met so far is **discriminative**: draw a boundary that separates classes. Generative AI asks for more: *learn what the data looks like*, so that you can produce new examples that are not in the training set. Figure 1 puts the two side by side on one data set. On the left, a discriminative model (logistic regression, blue) learns only a boundary. A generative model (red dashed boundary; it fits one Gaussian to each class) has *also* learned what each class looks like, so it can **draw new points** (black crosses) from the class it was told to imitate. On the right, the same two models are compared by how fast they learn. ![Left: two classes of points with the boundary found by a discriminative model (blue, a straight line) and by a generative model that fits one Gaussian per class (red dashed, curved). Black crosses are 25 NEW points drawn from the fitted model of the orange class: only a generative model can do this. Right: test accuracy against the number of training points per class (mean of 60 runs).](figures/fig01_disc_gen.png){width=95%} The right-hand panel contradicts a common slogan, so read it carefully. With only 3 training points per class the generative model is *worse* (72.5% against 87.0%): it estimates a full covariance matrix for each class from three points. The two cross at about 10 points per class. With plenty of data the generative model is slightly *better* (92.5% against 91.8% at 300 points per class), because its assumption (each class is Gaussian) happens to be true here, whereas the straight-line boundary of logistic regression cannot follow the curved optimal boundary. Which wins depends on how many parameters the generative model must estimate and on whether its assumptions are right. ## 3.2 Tier 2 (medium): the definitions made precise ### 3.2.1 The goal of every generative model Given samples $x^{(1)},\\dots,x^{(N)}$ from an unknown data distribution $p_{\\text{data}}$, build a model $p_\\theta$ that (a) you can sample from, and ideally (b) assigns a **likelihood** $p_\\theta(x)$ to any $x$. The standard way to fit it is **maximum likelihood**: choose $\\theta$ to maximise $\\sum_i\\ln p_\\theta(x^{(i)})$. The six families differ in *how they make $p_\\theta$ tractable*. ### 3.2.2 Autoregressive models The chain rule of probability lets you write any distribution over a sequence as a product, $$ p_\\theta(x_{1:n})=\\prod_{i=1}^{n}p_\\theta(x_i\\mid x_{1:i-1}), $$ {#eq:ar} the same equation as in Unit 3. Each factor is a distribution over *one* symbol, which is easy to model. Generation is a loop: sample $x_1$, then $x_2$ given $x_1$, and so on. Likelihoods are exact. Cost: generation is sequential. The simplest instance is the **character $n$-gram model**: the context is only the last $n-1$ characters, and the probability is a ratio of counts, $$ \\hat p(c\\mid \\text{ctx})=\\frac{\\#(\\text{ctx},c)+\\alpha}{\\#(\\text{ctx})+\\alpha\\,(V+1)}, $$ {#eq:ngram} with a small smoothing constant $\\alpha$ (here $0.05$) so that unseen combinations do not get probability $0$, and $V$ the number of distinct characters. This is the \"build a simple text-generation model in Python\" practical of the syllabus: the module's `CharNgram` has 40 lines. Trained on the course syllabus (6{,}941 letters and spaces, 27 distinct characters, first 80% for training), it generates: | order $n$ | sample of 110 characters | |:--|:--| | 1 | `uiv neratltigrwevtanoun iiiuebkntmdtiniaoraieaautep tlsdeevgiutfer ...` | | 2 | `sexindietitinntext moun lintecontiltionarodalaivini tis ffulivondichttabunciconiconeenge ...` | | 4 | `sure former building vs gent evances practical to shot build bpe self attent summariation and moderation modal` | | 6 | `sure for example text to image systems agent evaluation frameworks deep learning instruction and logging overv` | Table: samples from the same data at four context lengths (seed fixed). Order 1 produces letter soup, order 4 produces *plausible-looking* nonsense (`summariation`), order 6 reads like the syllabus. The last row is not creativity; Section 3.4 shows why. ![Perplexity of the character $n$-gram model on the training text (blue) and on held-out text (red dashed), by order $n$. Training perplexity keeps falling (17.65 at $n=1$, 2.08 at $n=6$). Held-out perplexity falls to a minimum of 7.65 at $n=4$ and then rises again (8.86 at $n=5$, 10.93 at $n=6$).](figures/fig02_ngram.png){width=70%} Figure 2 is the first picture of **overfitting**: more context makes the model better on the text it has seen and *worse* on new text. A model that has memorised cannot generalise. ### 3.2.3 Autoencoders and latent variables An **autoencoder** has an encoder $e_\\phi:\\mathbb{R}^{D}\\to\\mathbb{R}^{k}$ with $k<D$ and a decoder $d_\\theta:\\mathbb{R}^{k}\\to\\mathbb{R}^{D}$, trained to minimise $$ \\mathcal{L}_{AE}=\\frac1N\\sum_i\\big\\lVert x^{(i)}-d_\\theta\\big(e_\\phi(x^{(i)})\\big)\\big\\rVert^2 . $$ {#eq:ae} The short vector $z=e_\\phi(x)$ is a **latent variable**: a compressed, hidden description of the data. The bottleneck forces the network to keep only what matters. A plain autoencoder is a *compressor*, not a generator: nothing says which codes $z$ are valid, so decoding a random $z$ gives garbage. Figure 3 shows what it does learn. ![Data on a noisy arc (grey). A nonlinear autoencoder with a one-number code (blue) reconstructs the arc with mean squared error $5.3\\times10^{-4}$; PCA with one component (red line) can only project onto a straight line and has error $0.275$, which is 520 times larger. Right: the learned code $z$ is almost a straight function of the true angle (correlation $-0.997$).](figures/fig03_ae.png){width=95%} ## 3.3 Tier 3 (medium-hard): the main derivations ### 3.3.1 The VAE and its ELBO A VAE adds the missing ingredient: a **prior** $p(z)=\\mathcal{N}(0,I)$ over codes, so that sampling is possible (draw $z\\sim p(z)$, decode). The model is $p_\\theta(x)=\\int p_\\theta(x\\mid z)\\,p(z)\\,dz$. This integral is intractable, so introduce an encoder distribution $q_\\phi(z\\mid x)$ as a stand-in for the true posterior $p_\\theta(z\\mid x)$ and derive a bound that *can* be computed. Start from the definition of $\\ln p_\\theta(x)$ and insert $q_\\phi$: $$ \\begin{aligned} \\ln p_\\theta(x)&=\\mathbb{E}_{q_\\phi(z\\mid x)}\\big[\\ln p_\\theta(x)\\big] &&\\text{(it does not depend on } z)\\\\ &=\\mathbb{E}_{q}\\Big[\\ln\\frac{p_\\theta(x,z)}{p_\\theta(z\\mid x)}\\Big] &&\\text{(product rule: } p(x,z)=p(z\\mid x)p(x))\\\\ &=\\mathbb{E}_{q}\\Big[\\ln\\frac{p_\\theta(x,z)}{q_\\phi(z\\mid x)}\\Big]+\\mathbb{E}_{q}\\Big[\\ln\\frac{q_\\phi(z\\mid x)}{p_\\theta(z\\mid x)}\\Big] &&\\text{(multiply and divide by } q)\\\\ &=\\mathcal{L}(\\theta,\\phi;x)+D_{KL}\\big(q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\,\\big\\Vert\\,p_\\theta(z\\mid x)\\big). \\end{aligned} $$ {#eq:elbo1} This is Eq. (1) of Kingma and Welling. The KL divergence is never negative, so $\\ln p_\\theta(x)\\ge\\mathcal{L}$: $\\mathcal{L}$ is a **lower bound**, the *evidence lower bound* (ELBO). Expanding $p(x,z)=p(x\\mid z)p(z)$ in the first term gives the form used in practice: $$ \\mathcal{L}(\\theta,\\phi;x)=\\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}_{q_\\phi(z\\mid x)}\\big[\\ln p_\\theta(x\\mid z)\\big]}_{\\text{reconstruction}}-\\underbrace{D_{KL}\\big(q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\,\\Vert\\,p(z)\\big)}_{\\text{keep codes close to the prior}} . $$ {#eq:elbo2} **Two choices make @eq:elbo2 concrete.** Take the encoder to be a diagonal Gaussian, $q_\\phi(z\\mid x)=\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\mathrm{diag}\\,\\sigma^2)$, with $\\mu,\\ln\\sigma^2$ output by the encoder network, and the decoder Gaussian with fixed variance $\\sigma_x^2$. *The KL term in closed form.* For one coordinate, with $p=\\mathcal{N}(0,1)$, $$ \\begin{aligned} D_{KL}\\big(\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\\,\\Vert\\,\\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\big)&=\\mathbb{E}_q\\Big[\\ln\\frac{q(z)}{p(z)}\\Big]=\\mathbb{E}_q\\Big[-\\tfrac12\\ln\\sigma^2-\\tfrac{(z-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}+\\tfrac{z^2}{2}\\Big]\\\\ &=-\\tfrac12\\ln\\sigma^2-\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12\\big(\\sigma^2+\\mu^2\\big)\\qquad\\big(\\mathbb{E}[(z-\\mu)^2]=\\sigma^2,\\ \\mathbb{E}[z^2]=\\sigma^2+\\mu^2\\big)\\\\ &=\\tfrac12\\big(\\mu^2+\\sigma^2-1-\\ln\\sigma^2\\big). \\end{aligned} $$ {#eq:kl} Worked check: $\\mu=1$, $\\sigma=0.5$ gives $\\tfrac12(1+0.25-1-\\ln0.25)=\\tfrac12(0.25+1.3863)=0.818$. The module's loss has the value $0$ when the encoder outputs $\\mu=0,\\ \\ln\\sigma^2=0$ (test `test_kl_is_zero_for_standard_normal_posterior`). *The reconstruction term.* With $p_\\theta(x\\mid z)=\\mathcal{N}(\\hat x(z),\\sigma_x^2I)$, $-\\ln p_\\theta(x\\mid z)=\\lVert x-\\hat x\\rVert^2/(2\\sigma_x^2)+\\text{const}$. So **minimising $-\\mathcal{L}$ is minimising** $$ \\frac{\\lVert x-\\hat x\\rVert^2}{2\\sigma_x^2}+\\tfrac12\\sum_j\\big(\\mu_j^2+\\sigma_j^2-1-\\ln\\sigma_j^2\\big). $$ {#eq:vaeloss} The first term is the autoencoder loss; the second keeps the codes close to $\\mathcal{N}(0,I)$. $\\sigma_x^2$ sets the trade-off: a small $\\sigma_x^2$ weights reconstruction heavily. **Reparameterisation.** The expectation in @eq:elbo2 is over $z\\sim q_\\phi$, which depends on $\\phi$; a sampled $z$ is a random number, and gradients cannot pass through \"sample\". The trick (Kingma and Welling, Section 2.4): write $z=\\mu+\\sigma\\odot\\epsilon$ with $\\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I)$ drawn *independently of the parameters*. Now $z$ is a deterministic, differentiable function of $(\\mu,\\sigma)$ given $\\epsilon$, and ordinary backpropagation works. The module implements it exactly that way, and the test `test_vae_loss_gradients_match_finite_differences` checks the hand-derived gradients of @eq:vaeloss against numerical differentiation. ![VAE with a two-dimensional code trained on 400 points of a noisy ring ($\\sigma_x^2=0.02$, 4000 steps). Left: points decoded from random codes $z\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I)$ (blue) fall on the data ring (grey), with some stray points inside it. Middle: the two loss terms per data point; the KL settles at 2.7 and the reconstruction term at about 0.55. Right: the encoder maps the ring to a closed loop in code space, coloured by angle.](figures/fig04_vae.png){width=95%} How good are the samples? The data ring has radius $1.001\\pm0.038$; the VAE samples have radius $0.944\\pm0.119$: on average slightly inside the ring and three times more spread out. That is the familiar **blurriness** of VAEs: the prior fills the code space, including regions the decoder was never trained on. ### 3.3.2 The GAN and its optimal discriminator A GAN has a generator $G:z\\mapsto x$ with $z\\sim p_z$ and a discriminator $D:x\\mapsto(0,1)$, the probability that $x$ is real. They play the game $$ \\min_G\\max_D\\ V(D,G)=\\mathbb{E}_{x\\sim p_{\\text{data}}}[\\ln D(x)]+\\mathbb{E}_{z\\sim p_z}[\\ln(1-D(G(z)))]. $$ {#eq:gan} **Optimal discriminator (Proposition 1).** Fix $G$ and let $p_g$ be the distribution of $G(z)$. Then $V=\\int\\big[p_{\\text{data}}(x)\\ln D(x)+p_g(x)\\ln(1-D(x))\\big]dx$. Maximise the integrand pointwise: for $a=p_{\\text{data}}(x)$ and $b=p_g(x)$, the function $f(D)=a\\ln D+b\\ln(1-D)$ has $$ f'(D)=\\frac aD-\\frac b{1-D}=0\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ D^{\\ast}(x)=\\frac{p_{\\text{data}}(x)}{p_{\\text{data}}(x)+p_g(x)}, $$ {#eq:dstar} and $f''<0$, so this is the maximum. Worked check: where $p_{\\text{data}}=0.3$ and $p_g=0.1$, $D^\\ast=0.75$: three quarters of the time a point there is real. Substituting back, $$ C(G)=\\max_DV(D,G)=-\\ln4+2\\,\\mathrm{JSD}\\big(p_{\\text{data}}\\,\\Vert\\,p_g\\big) $$ {#eq:jsd} (Eq. (5) of Goodfellow et al.), where JSD is the Jensen-Shannon divergence, which is $\\ge0$ and zero only if $p_g=p_{\\text{data}}$. So the generator's best possible outcome is $C=-\\ln4\\approx-1.386$ (their Theorem 1), reached when $D^\\ast\\equiv\\tfrac12$: the discriminator can do no better than guessing. **Practice differs from theory.** Early in training $D$ rejects fakes confidently, so $\\ln(1-D(G(z)))$ is flat and gives $G$ almost no gradient. The paper's remedy, used in the module, is the **non-saturating loss**: train $G$ to *maximise* $\\ln D(G(z))$ instead. Figure 5 shows a 1-D GAN learning a two-bump target. ![A 1-D GAN (two hidden layers of width 24, code size 2) learning a target made of two Gaussians at $-2$ and $+2$ (grey). At step 0 all generated points are near 0 (standard deviation 0.29). At step 300 both modes are found: left mode mean $-1.95$, standard deviation 0.37; right mode $1.98$, 0.40; targets are $\\pm2$ and $0.4$. At step 1500 and step 4000 both modes are still covered (96% of samples within 1 of a mode at step 4000) but the histograms have become spiky and the modes have drifted to $\\mp1.7$.](figures/fig05_gan.png){width=95%} Read the picture honestly. The best match to the target is at step 300, not at the end: **GAN training does not converge monotonically**, because two networks chase each other, and no single loss curve tells you when the samples are best. Here the generator keeps both modes; a generator that covers only one is called **mode collapse**, one of the known failure modes. ### 3.3.3 Diffusion in one paragraph A diffusion model fixes a *forward* process that adds a little Gaussian noise at each of $T$ steps, $x_t=\\sqrt{1-\\beta_t}\\,x_{t-1}+\\sqrt{\\beta_t}\\,\\epsilon_t$, and trains a network to undo it. With $\\bar\\alpha_t=\\prod_{s\\le t}(1-\\beta_s)$ the noisy sample can be written directly in terms of the clean one, $$ x_t=\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_t}\\,x_0+\\sqrt{1-\\bar\\alpha_t}\\,\\epsilon,\\qquad\\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I). $$ {#eq:diff} Ho et al. use $T=1000$ with $\\beta_t$ rising linearly from $10^{-4}$ to $0.02$ (Section 4). Computing the product for that schedule gives $\\bar\\alpha_{100}=0.897$ (almost no noise yet), $\\bar\\alpha_{500}=0.079$, and $\\bar\\alpha_{1000}=4.0\\times10^{-5}$, so $\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{1000}}=0.0064$: the clean signal is scaled down to under $1\\%$ of its original amplitude and what remains is almost pure noise, which is where the reverse process starts. The full derivation, the training loss and a runnable example are in Unit 7. ## 3.4 Tier 4 (hard): comparing the families, and the four risks ### 3.4.1 Side by side | | autoregressive | VAE | GAN | diffusion | |:--|:--|:--|:--|:--| | likelihood | exact | lower bound (ELBO) | none | bound | | sampling | one step per symbol, sequential | one forward pass | one forward pass | many steps ($T=1000$ in the original paper) | | training | stable, plain cross-entropy | stable, often blurry | adversarial, can oscillate (Fig. 5) | stable, expensive | | typical failure | repetition, hallucination | blur | mode collapse | slow sampling | Table: a summary of the trade-offs derived above. The statement about diffusion sampling cost is for the original paper's setting; faster samplers exist and are covered in Unit 7. The Transformer is not a row: it is the neural network inside, most often, an autoregressive model. ### 3.4.2 Hallucination, bias, privacy, copyright All four risks follow from **what the training objective rewards**. - **Hallucination.** The objective is the likelihood of the training text, not its truth. A model is rewarded for *plausible* continuations. Section 3.2.2 already shows the effect in miniature: order 4 invents `summariation` and `evances`, which have the statistical look of English words and none of the meaning. - **Bias.** A model fitted to a distribution reproduces that distribution, including its imbalances and stereotypes. Generative models cannot be more balanced than their data without an extra step. - **Privacy and copyright through memorisation.** A model with enough capacity can reproduce its training data. In the $n$-gram model this is measurable: take a 3000-character sample and count the share of its 12-character windows that occur *verbatim* in the training text. | order $n$ | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 6 | 8 | |:--|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:| | verbatim 12-character windows | 0.0% | 0.0% | 0.1% | 6.9% | 60.0% | 88.8% | Table: memorisation in the character $n$-gram model (from `fig02_ngram.py`). It rises together with the *held-out* perplexity in Figure 2: the same capacity that lets the model copy hurts it on new text. Large neural models are different machines, but the mechanism is the same, so training data that contains private or copyrighted text can come back out. - **Safety.** A model that can generate anything can generate harmful content. Mitigations (filtering data, tuning the model, filtering outputs) are the subject of Unit 9. ## 3.5 Tier 5 (hardest): open questions 1. **Can a model know when it is hallucinating?** The likelihood a model assigns to a sentence is not a reliable signal of whether it is true. Whether a model's internal state can tell \"I know this\" from \"I am guessing\", and whether that can be made reliable, is an active research question. 2. **Is good likelihood the same as good samples?** A model can assign high likelihood to data and still produce poor samples, and the reverse (GANs have no likelihood at all yet produced sharp images). How to evaluate generative models without a single trusted number is unsettled. 3. **How much memorisation is acceptable?** There is no agreed threshold at which reproducing training text becomes a privacy or copyright problem, and measures like the one in Section 3.4.2 depend on the window size chosen. # 4. Apply ## 4.1 Worked problems **Problem 1 (Bayes classification).** Two classes with priors $0.5$ each; at a point $x$ the class-conditional densities are $p(x\\mid A)=0.2$ and $p(x\\mid B)=0.05$. By @eq:bayes, $p(A\\mid x)=\\frac{0.2\\cdot0.5}{0.2\\cdot0.5+0.05\\cdot0.5}=0.8$. **Problem 2 (KL).** A VAE encoder outputs $\\mu=1$, $\\sigma=0.5$ for one coordinate. By @eq:kl the KL to the prior is $0.818$ nats. **Problem 3 (optimal discriminator).** At a point where $p_{\\text{data}}=0.3$ and $p_g=0.1$, $D^\\ast=0.3/0.4=0.75$. If the generator later matches the data, $D^\\ast=0.5$ everywhere. **Problem 4 ($n$-gram).** In context `th` suppose the training text has `e` 8 times, `a` twice, nothing else. With $\\alpha=0$, $\\hat p(e\\mid th)=0.8$ and the loss contribution of a true `e` is $-\\ln0.8=0.223$ nats; of a true `i` (never seen) it would be infinite, which is why smoothing is needed. **Problem 5 (diffusion).** For $x_0=1$ and $\\bar\\alpha_t=0.5$, @eq:diff gives $x_t=0.707+0.707\\,\\epsilon$: mean $0.707$, standard deviation $0.707$. ## 4.2 Code and figures Everything is in `codes/genmodels/genmodels_np/` (see its README). The generator loop of the $n$-gram model: ```python def generate(self, length, rng, seed_text=\"\"): ctx = (\"\\x00\" * (self.n - 1) + seed_text)[-(self.n - 1):] for _ in range(length): counts = self.counts.get(ctx) # distribution over the next character chars, cnt = zip(*sorted(counts.items())) ch = chars[rng.choice(len(chars), p=np.array(cnt) / sum(cnt))] out.append(ch); ctx = (ctx + ch)[-(self.n - 1):] # slide the context window ``` and the VAE gradient, derived by hand and verified numerically: ```python dz, gd = dec.backward(cd, r / sigma2 / B) # through the decoder dmu = dz + mu / B # + d(KL)/d(mu) dlogv = dz * eps * 0.5 * std + 0.5 * (np.exp(logv) - 1) / B ``` All five figure scripts print the numbers quoted in this document. ::: {.keyidea title=\"Try it\"} The code zip lets you change `n` in `CharNgram`, `sigma2` in `train_vae`, or the real distribution in `train_gan_1d`, and rerun the figure scripts. The playground's *Text generator (n-gram)* widget runs the same model in your browser, and its *Generative classifier* widget lets you move the two Gaussians of Figure 1 yourself. The Unit 3 *Next-token sampler* shows the decoding side of an autoregressive model. ::: # 5. Analyze *Checklist items used: limits (5.1), dominance (5.2), sensitivity (5.3), failure modes (5.4), invariants and gradient checks (5.5).* ## 5.1 Limits - $\\alpha\\to\\infty$ in @eq:ngram: every character becomes equally likely (uniform), and the model ignores the data. - $\\sigma_x^2\\to\\infty$ in @eq:vaeloss: reconstruction is ignored and the encoder collapses to the prior (KL $\\to0$): the code carries no information. - $\\sigma_x^2\\to0$: reconstruction dominates and the VAE approaches a plain autoencoder. - $p_g=p_{\\text{data}}$ in the GAN: $D^\\ast\\equiv\\tfrac12$ and $C=-\\ln4$. ## 5.2 Which term dominates In the trained VAE of Figure 4, the KL term (2.7) is about five times the reconstruction term (0.55) per data point, so the prior pull is strong; raising $\\sigma_x^2$ would raise the share of KL further. ## 5.3 Sensitivity Figure 2 is a sensitivity plot in $n$: the held-out perplexity changes by $43\\%$ between $n=4$ (7.65) and $n=6$ (10.93). The GAN of Figure 5 is sensitive in *time*: the best step was 300 of 4000. ## 5.4 Failure modes - **Memorisation** in autoregressive models (60% verbatim windows at $n=6$). - **Blur and stray samples** in VAEs (Figure 4: sample radius spread $0.119$ against $0.038$ for the data). - **Instability and drift** in GANs (Figure 5). - **Compression without structure** in plain autoencoders: decoding a code that was never produced by the encoder gives no guarantee. ## 5.5 Checks that the code is right - `test_mlp_backprop_matches_finite_differences` and `test_vae_loss_gradients_match_finite_differences`: hand-written backpropagation agrees with numerical differentiation to relative error $10^{-5}$ or better. - `test_kl_is_zero_for_standard_normal_posterior`: the KL is exactly zero at $\\mu=0,\\ln\\sigma^2=0$. - `test_autoencoder_beats_pca_on_a_curve`, `test_gan_moves_toward_the_real_mean`, `test_ngram_prob_sums_to_one_and_train_perplexity_falls_with_order`: the behaviours described above hold. # 6. Evaluate **Choosing a family.** For text and code the autoregressive Transformer is the standard (Units 2 and 3); for images the leading systems have mostly been diffusion-based (Unit 7); VAEs are useful when you need a smooth, low-dimensional latent space; GANs are the fast-sampling option when you can afford careful training. This is a snapshot, not a law: the ranking has changed several times since 2013 and will change again. **Generative versus discriminative in practice.** If you only need a label and have plenty of data, a discriminative model is usually simpler and at least as accurate (Figure 1, right, and the reasoning around it). Choose a generative model when you need samples, a density, or to handle missing data. **When not to trust a generative model.** When correctness matters more than plausibility (medical, legal, financial claims), unless the output is checked or grounded in sources (Unit 5); when the training data contain sensitive personal text; when a single number such as perplexity is used to claim that samples are good. **Wider context.** The distinction between *learning to discriminate* and *learning to generate* is old in statistics, and so is the debate over which is more fundamental. Generative AI inherits both the power and the unresolved questions: a system that can produce anything is only as trustworthy as the process that checks it. # 7. Create ::: {.create title=\"What you can build from this\"} 1. **Your own text generator:** train `CharNgram` on a book chapter you choose; plot train versus test perplexity by order and pick the order that minimises held-out perplexity. 2. **Memorisation meter:** extend the verbatim-window measure to word windows and to several window lengths, and plot the curve for orders 2 to 10. 3. **Latent-space walk:** train the VAE on a ring or a spiral, then decode a straight line of codes between two points and plot the path. 4. **GAN stability study:** run `train_gan_1d` with 20 seeds and report how often both modes are found at step 300 versus step 4000. 5. **Combine with Unit 3:** replace the character $n$-gram by a *word* model using the BPE tokenizer of session 001 and compare perplexities. 6. **Module for the open-source package:** add a `beta` weight on the KL term to `train_vae` and test that $\\beta=0$ gives the autoencoder. ::: # 8. Visualization lesson | Chart type | Best for | How it misleads | matplotlib call | |:--|:--|:--|:--| | scatter with decision contour | classes and a boundary (Fig. 1 left) | the boundary looks certain; it is only the 50% line | `ax.contour(X, Y, Z, levels=[.5])` | | learning curve (accuracy against training size, log x) | data efficiency (Fig. 1 right) | averages hide run-to-run spread | `ax.semilogx(n, acc, \"o-\")` | | train versus held-out curve | overfitting (Fig. 2) | a held-out set that is too small makes the curve noisy | `ax.semilogy(order, ptr); ax.semilogy(order, pte)` | | small multiples of histograms | a distribution changing over training (Fig. 5) | different bin widths change the apparent shape; here all share bins and the y-axis | `plt.subplots(1, 4, sharey=True)` and `ax.hist(..., range=(-4.5, 4.5))` | | scatter coloured by a variable | which part of the input maps where (Fig. 4 right) | a cyclic variable such as an angle needs a *cyclic* colormap | `ax.scatter(x, y, c=angle, cmap=\"twilight\")` | Table: charts taught here. How to read the small multiples in Figure 5: compare each blue histogram with the grey target in the same panel and look at the *shape*, not just the position: the mean can be right while the spread is wrong. # 9. Problem set ::: {.exercise title=\"Graded exercises\"} 1. (easy) State one task where you need a generative model and one where a discriminative model is enough. 2. (medium) Compute the KL divergence of $\\mathcal{N}(2,1)$ from $\\mathcal{N}(0,1)$ using @eq:kl, and of $\\mathcal{N}(0,4)$ from $\\mathcal{N}(0,1)$. 3. (medium-hard) Show that $f(D)=a\\ln D+b\\ln(1-D)$ with $a,b>0$ is maximised at $D=a/(a+b)$, and that the maximum value is $a\\ln\\frac a{a+b}+b\\ln\\frac b{a+b}$. 4. (hard) Derive @eq:diff from the one-step rule $x_t=\\sqrt{1-\\beta_t}\\,x_{t-1}+\\sqrt{\\beta_t}\\,\\epsilon_t$ for $t=2$ and generalise. (Hint: sums of independent Gaussians are Gaussian; add the variances.) 5. (hardest) With $\\alpha=0.05$ and $V=27$, compute from @eq:ngram the total probability the smoothed model assigns to characters *never seen* after a context that occurred $m=10$ times followed by 3 distinct characters, and again for $m=1$ followed by 1 character. Explain why this makes smoothing matter more as the order $n$ grows. (Answer: $24\\cdot0.05/11.4=0.105$ and $26\\cdot0.05/2.4=0.542$.) ::: # 10. Retrieval practice and recap **Answers to the Section 2 quiz.** 1. $p(x)$ (or $p(x\\mid y)$); $p(y\\mid x)$ alone only labels. 2. The previous symbols: all of them in the chain rule, the last $n-1$ in an $n$-gram. 3. Reconstruction error $\\lVert x-\\hat x\\rVert^2/(2\\sigma_x^2)$ and the KL term $\\tfrac12\\sum(\\mu^2+\\sigma^2-1-\\ln\\sigma^2)$. 4. $\\tfrac12$ everywhere: it cannot tell real from generated. 5. Memorisation (reproducing training text, Section 3.4.2) and hallucination or bias reflected from the data; both follow from what the objective rewards. **Explain it to a friend.** - Why can a generative classifier draw new examples but logistic regression cannot? - Why does adding more context to an $n$-gram model first help and then hurt on new text? - What is the trick that lets gradients flow through random sampling in a VAE? **Re-derive tomorrow without looking:** (a) $D^\\ast=p_{\\text{data}}/(p_{\\text{data}}+p_g)$; (b) the VAE KL formula @eq:kl; (c) $\\ln p(x)=\\mathcal{L}+D_{KL}(q\\Vert p(z\\mid x))$. # 11. Where next - **Next node:** Unit 4 (prompt engineering and LLM applications), which uses the decoding settings from session 001 and the model built in session 002. - **Terms met but not taught:** classifier-free guidance, flow matching, energy-based models (named in surveys; not needed for this course). - **Open threads:** what makes a GAN's best step 300 rather than 4000 (Figure 5; not resolved here); how to measure memorisation in large models (Section 3.5).", "u2": "# Session map ::: {.keyidea title=\"Outcome of this session\"} After this session you can: 1. explain why recurrent networks struggle with long sequences, with a measured number, not a slogan; 2. compute scaled dot-product attention by hand for a small example, and say what every symbol means; 3. derive why attention divides by $\\sqrt{d_k}$, why positional encoding is needed, and why the sinusoidal one gives *relative* offsets; 4. count the parameters and FLOPs of a Transformer block from its shape, and find the sequence length where attention starts to dominate the cost; 5. say which of BERT, GPT and T5 is an encoder, a decoder, or both, and why that decides what each is good at. ::: - **Prerequisites:** matrices and vectors, the dot product, softmax (Unit 3, Section 2), a first neural-network course. Session 001 (Unit 3) used the Transformer only as a black box that outputs logits; this session opens the box. - **Difficulty ladder used:** easy \u2192 medium \u2192 medium-hard \u2192 hard (Sections 3.1 to 3.4); the hardest tier is posed as open questions in Section 3.5. - **Deliverables:** this document (.tex, .pdf, .docx), 6 figures, the module `codes/llm/transformer_np/` (attention, multi-head, positional encoding, a full block, RNN gradients, FLOP counts; 9 tests), and the *Attention heatmap* and *Positional encoding* widgets in the LLM Studio playground. - **Source anchors:** Vaswani et al. 2017, arXiv:1706.03762 (Sections 3.1 to 3.5 and Table 1 for architecture, scaling by $\\sqrt{d_k}$, sinusoidal encoding, complexity; Table 3 for the base-model sizes); Devlin et al. 2018, arXiv:1810.04805 (BERT); Raffel et al. 2019, arXiv:1910.10683 (T5); Su et al. 2021, arXiv:2104.09864 (rotary position embedding). All IDs were checked against arXiv on the day of writing, and the numbers quoted from Vaswani et al. were read from the paper's PDF. # 1. Terms: the term tree | Term | Symbol | One-line meaning | Where it appears | New / known | |:-----|:------:|:-----------------|:-----------------|:-----------:| | sequence model | | a model whose input and output are ordered lists | 3.1 | new | | recurrent neural network (RNN) | $h_t$ | reads a sequence step by step, carrying a hidden state | 3.2 | new | | hidden state | $h_t$ | the RNN's running summary of everything read so far | 3.2 | new | | vanishing gradient | | gradients shrink exponentially with distance | 3.2 | new | | attention | | a weighted average of values, with weights from query-key similarity | 3.2 | new | | query, key, value | $Q,K,V$ | what I look for / what I offer / what I hand over | 3.2 | new | | self-attention | | queries, keys and values all come from the same sequence | 3.2 | new | | cross-attention | | queries from one sequence, keys and values from another | 3.2 | new | | multi-head attention | $h$ | several attention operations in parallel, then mixed | 3.3 | new | | positional encoding | $PE$ | vectors added to embeddings so the model can tell order | 3.3 | new | | causal mask | | forbids attending to later positions | 3.2 | new | | residual connection | | output = input + sub-layer(input) | 3.3 | new | | layer normalisation | | rescales each token vector to zero mean and unit variance | 3.3 | new | | feed-forward network (FFN) | $d_{ff}$ | the per-token two-layer MLP inside each block | 3.3 | new | | encoder / decoder | | bidirectional reader / left-to-right writer | 3.4 | new | Terms *used but not taught here*: SentencePiece and tokenization details (Unit 3), the KV cache (Unit 10), instruction tuning (Unit 6). # 2. Remember ::: {.definition title=\"Attention\"} Given queries $Q\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_q\\times d_k}$, keys $K\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_k\\times d_k}$ and values $V\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n_k\\times d_v}$, $$ \\mathrm{Attention}(Q,K,V)=\\mathrm{softmax}\\!\\Big(\\frac{QK^{\\top}}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\Big)V. $$ {#eq:attn} Row $i$ of the softmax is a probability distribution over the $n_k$ positions: how much query $i$ reads from each. The output is that weighted average of the value rows. ::: ::: {.definition title=\"Multi-head attention\"} Split the width $d$ into $h$ heads of size $d_k=d/h$. Each head has its own projection matrices $W^Q_i,W^K_i,W^V_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d_k}$. The heads' outputs are concatenated and mixed by $W^O\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d}$: $$ \\mathrm{MHA}(x)=\\big[\\mathrm{head}_1;\\dots;\\mathrm{head}_h\\big]W^O,\\qquad \\mathrm{head}_i=\\mathrm{Attention}(xW^Q_i,\\,xW^K_i,\\,xW^V_i). $$ ::: ::: {.definition title=\"Sinusoidal positional encoding\"} For position $pos$ and pair index $i=0,\\dots,d/2-1$ (Vaswani et al., Section 3.5): $$ PE_{pos,2i}=\\sin\\!\\big(pos/10000^{2i/d}\\big),\\qquad PE_{pos,2i+1}=\\cos\\!\\big(pos/10000^{2i/d}\\big). $$ {#eq:pe} ::: ::: {.definition title=\"A Transformer block (pre-norm form)\"} $$ \\begin{aligned} x' &= x + \\mathrm{MHA}(\\mathrm{LN}(x)),\\\\ y &= x' + \\mathrm{FFN}(\\mathrm{LN}(x')),\\qquad \\mathrm{FFN}(u)=\\phi(uW_1+b_1)W_2+b_2, \\end{aligned} $$ {#eq:block} with $W_1\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d_{ff}}$, $W_2\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{ff}\\times d}$, usually $d_{ff}=4d$. (The original paper puts the layer norm *after* the residual sum; the pre-norm form above is what most current models use.) ::: **Memory hooks.** - *Library.* The **query** is your question, each **key** is the label on a shelf, each **value** is the book. You read a blend of all books, weighted by how well each label matches your question. - *Heads are committee members.* Each head looks for a different kind of relation; the output matrix $W^O$ is the chair who merges their reports. - *Block = communicate, then think.* Attention moves information *between* tokens; the FFN processes each token *alone*. **Recall quiz** (answers in Section 10). 1. What are the shapes of $QK^{\\top}$ and of the attention output in @eq:attn? 2. Why is there a $\\sqrt{d_k}$ in the denominator? 3. What does the Transformer block do to the *order* of the tokens if no positional information is added? 4. What does a causal mask forbid? 5. Which of BERT, GPT, T5 uses cross-attention? # 3. Understand: the derivation ## 3.1 Tier 1 (easy): intuition and a picture A language task is a **sequence** problem: the meaning of a word depends on other words, possibly far away. In \"The animal didn't cross the street because *it* was tired\", what does *it* refer to? To answer, the representation of \"it\" has to *look back* at \"animal\". Before 2017 the standard tool was the **recurrent network**: read the words one at a time, keep a running memory (the hidden state), update it at each word. Two problems follow. (1) It is **sequential**: step $t$ cannot start before step $t-1$ finishes, so it cannot use a GPU's parallelism over the sequence. (2) Information from the start has to survive many updates to reach the end. **Attention** removes both problems with one idea: let every position look *directly* at every other position and take a weighted average of what it finds. The weights are computed from similarity. Figure 1 shows the result for a toy sentence. ![Attention weights for a hand-built seven-token example (the vectors are set by hand to make the point, not learned). Rows are the token that is reading, columns the token being read. In both panels the row for \"it\" puts 0.98 of its weight on \"animal\". On the right a causal mask (grey zeros above the diagonal) forbids looking at later words.](figures/fig01_attention.png){width=90%} How to read it: each **row** sums to 1. Row \"it\" is almost one dark cell: \"it\" copies information from \"animal\". Row \"The\" on the left is a flat stripe: its query is zero, so every score is equal and it averages everything (each weight $1/7=0.143$). On the right, row \"The\" is a single dark cell, because the mask leaves it only itself to read. ## 3.2 Tier 2 (medium): the definitions made precise ### 3.2.1 The recurrent network and its limits An RNN keeps a hidden state $h_t\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ and updates it with the new input $x_t$: $$ h_t=\\tanh\\!\\big(W h_{t-1}+U x_t\\big). $$ {#eq:rnn} An LSTM (Hochreiter and Schmidhuber, 1997) adds gates that decide what to keep and what to overwrite, which helps, but the loop structure remains: $h_t$ depends on $h_{t-1}$. To learn a dependency between positions $0$ and $t$, the gradient must flow back through $t$ steps. By the chain rule, with Jacobians $J_s=\\partial h_{s+1}/\\partial h_s=\\mathrm{diag}(1-h_{s+1}^2)\\,W$, $$ \\frac{\\partial h_t}{\\partial h_0}=J_{t-1}J_{t-2}\\cdots J_0 . $$ {#eq:jac} A product of $t$ matrices. If each stretches a typical vector by a factor $\\lambda<1$ the product shrinks like $\\lambda^{t}$ (**vanishing gradient**); if $\\lambda>1$ it grows like $\\lambda^{t}$ (exploding). Figure 2 measures it. ![Spectral norm of the Jacobian product in @eq:jac for a tanh RNN with $d=64$, as a function of the distance $t$ (log scale). Weight scale 0.6: the norm is 0.028 at $t=10$ and $2\\times10^{-9}$ at $t=50$. Scale 1.0: 1.43 and 3.73. Scale 1.6: 9.6 and 26.3. Self-attention connects any two positions in one step.](figures/fig03_rnn.png){width=80%} The honest reading of Figure 2 is *not* \"gradients always vanish\". For small weights they vanish by nine orders of magnitude in 50 steps; for larger weights the norm does not vanish at all, and at scale 1.6 it grows (the tanh saturates, which limits the growth). The point is that **the outcome depends on the weight scale in a way the training procedure does not control**, and the plotted number is the *largest* singular value, which says nothing about whether *useful* directions survive. Attention avoids the question: the path between two positions has length 1. ### 3.2.2 Attention, computed by hand Take one query $Q=(1,\\,0)$, two keys $K=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$ and values $V=\\begin{pmatrix}10&0\\\\0&10\\end{pmatrix}$, so $d_k=2$. $$ \\begin{aligned} \\text{scores}&=QK^{\\top}/\\sqrt{2}=(1,\\,0)/1.4142=(0.7071,\\ 0),\\\\ \\text{weights}&=\\mathrm{softmax}=\\Big(\\tfrac{e^{0.7071}}{e^{0.7071}+1},\\ \\tfrac{1}{e^{0.7071}+1}\\Big)=\\Big(\\tfrac{2.028}{3.028},\\ \\tfrac{1}{3.028}\\Big)=(0.670,\\ 0.330),\\\\ \\text{output}&=0.670\\,(10,0)+0.330\\,(0,10)=(6.698,\\ 3.302). \\end{aligned} $$ The query matches the first key, so the output is mostly the first value, but not entirely: attention is a *soft* lookup. The module's `attention(Q, K, V)` returns exactly these numbers. ### 3.2.3 Self-attention, cross-attention, masks In **self-attention** $Q$, $K$ and $V$ are all linear projections of the *same* sequence $x$: $Q=xW^Q$, $K=xW^K$, $V=xW^V$. In **cross-attention** the queries come from one sequence (for example the text being generated) and the keys and values from another (for example the encoded source sentence). The formula @eq:attn is identical. A **causal mask** sets the score of every forbidden pair to $-\\infty$ *before* the softmax, so its weight becomes exactly $0$. For a decoder that generates left to right, position $i$ may read only positions $j\\le i$; otherwise training would let the model cheat by reading the very token it must predict. ## 3.3 Tier 3 (medium-hard): the main derivations ### 3.3.1 Why divide by $\\sqrt{d_k}$ Let the components of a query $q$ and a key $k$ be independent with mean 0 and variance 1. The raw score is $s=q^{\\top}k=\\sum_{j=1}^{d_k}q_jk_j$. $$ \\begin{aligned} \\mathbb{E}[s]&=\\sum_j\\mathbb{E}[q_j]\\mathbb{E}[k_j]=0 &&\\text{(independence)}\\\\ \\mathrm{Var}(s)&=\\sum_j\\mathrm{Var}(q_jk_j)=\\sum_j\\mathbb{E}[q_j^2]\\,\\mathbb{E}[k_j^2]=d_k &&\\text{(terms are independent, each has variance 1)} \\end{aligned} $$ So the typical size of a score grows like $\\sqrt{d_k}$. Large scores push the softmax into saturation: one weight near 1, the rest near 0, and the gradient through the softmax is then nearly zero. Dividing by $\\sqrt{d_k}$ restores $\\mathrm{Var}(s/\\sqrt{d_k})=1$ for every $d_k$. ![Mean of the largest attention weight over 16 random keys, as $d_k$ grows. With the $1/\\sqrt{d_k}$ scaling (blue) it stays at 0.25 for every $d_k$. Without it (red) it climbs from 0.43 at $d_k=4$ to 0.99 at $d_k=4096$: the softmax becomes a hard argmax.](figures/fig06_sqrtdk.png){width=75%} The test `test_sqrt_dk_keeps_score_variance_one` checks the same fact numerically with $d_k=256$. ### 3.3.2 Multi-head attention costs no more than one big head With $h$ heads of width $d_k=d/h$, each head's projections are $d\\times d_k$, so all $3h$ of them together hold $3\\,h\\,d\\,d_k=3d^2$ weights, exactly what one full-width head would hold. The output matrix adds $d^2$. So **attention has $4d^2$ weights regardless of $h$**. What changes with $h$ is the *structure*: $h$ independent softmaxes, which lets different heads attend to different things at the same position. ### 3.3.3 Positional encoding, and why the sinusoid gives relative offsets Self-attention treats its input as a *set*: shuffle the tokens and the outputs are shuffled the same way (the model is permutation-equivariant), so \"dog bites man\" and \"man bites dog\" would look identical. Order must be injected. The original solution adds $PE_{pos}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ (@eq:pe) to the embedding of the token at position $pos$. Write $\\omega_i=10000^{-2i/d}$. The pair of entries for frequency $i$ is $(\\sin\\omega_i pos,\\ \\cos\\omega_i pos)$. By the angle-addition formulas, $$ \\begin{pmatrix}\\sin\\omega_i(pos+k)\\\\ \\cos\\omega_i(pos+k)\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\omega_ik&\\sin\\omega_ik\\\\ -\\sin\\omega_ik&\\cos\\omega_ik\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}\\sin\\omega_i\\,pos\\\\ \\cos\\omega_i\\,pos\\end{pmatrix}. $$ {#eq:rot} So $PE_{pos+k}$ equals a fixed **rotation** of $PE_{pos}$ that depends on the offset $k$ and *not* on $pos$. That is the property Vaswani et al. state in Section 3.5: a linear map takes $PE_{pos}$ to $PE_{pos+k}$. It also gives the dot product between two positions a clean form: $$ PE_{pos}\\!\\cdot\\!PE_{pos+k}=\\sum_{i=0}^{d/2-1}\\big[\\sin\\omega_i pos\\,\\sin\\omega_i(pos+k)+\\cos\\omega_i pos\\,\\cos\\omega_i(pos+k)\\big]=\\sum_{i=0}^{d/2-1}\\cos(\\omega_i k). $$ {#eq:pedot} It depends only on $k$. At $k=0$ it equals $d/2$ (each term is 1): for $d=64$ that is 32, and the code returns exactly $32.0$. At $k=10$ it is $21.05$, from every starting position (the spread over 100 starting positions is $2\\times10^{-14}$). ![Left: the positional-encoding matrix for 64 positions and 64 dimensions; low dimensions oscillate fast, high dimensions slowly. Right: the dot product between position $p$ and $p+k$ for three different starting positions $p$; the three curves lie exactly on top of each other, as @eq:pedot predicts.](figures/fig02_pe.png){width=90%} How much does order matter to a block? Figure 5 shuffles 8 tokens 20 times and measures how far the output moves from the correspondingly shuffled output of the unshuffled input. ![Largest difference after shuffling the token order (20 random shuffles, log scale). Without positional encoding the difference is $9\\times10^{-16}$ (rounding error only: the block cannot see order). With positional encoding added it is between 1.8 and 2.6.](figures/fig05_order.png){width=65%} ### 3.3.4 Residuals, layer norm, and the parameter count of a block The **residual connection** $x+f(x)$ gives gradients a direct path through every layer, so a stack of dozens of blocks stays trainable. **Layer normalisation** rescales each token vector to zero mean and unit variance, then applies a learned scale $g$ and shift $b$: $\\mathrm{LN}(x)=g\\odot(x-\\mu)/\\sqrt{\\sigma^2+\\varepsilon}+b$. Count the parameters of one block with width $d$ and $d_{ff}=4d$: | part | weights | biases | |:--|--:|--:| | attention $W^Q,W^K,W^V,W^O$ | $4d^2$ | $4d$ | | FFN $W_1$ ($d\\times4d$), $W_2$ ($4d\\times d$) | $8d^2$ | $4d+d$ | | two layer norms ($g$ and $b$ each) | | $4d$ | | **total** | $12d^2$ | $13d$ | Table: parameters of one block, $12d^2+13d$. The module's `count_params` on a block with $d=16$ returns $3280=12\\cdot256+13\\cdot16$, and the test `test_param_count_formula` checks $d=16$ and $d=64$. This is the $12Ld^2$ used in Unit 3 for a model of $L$ blocks. ## 3.4 Tier 4 (hard): cost, and the three Transformer families ### 3.4.1 Where the FLOPs go Count one multiply-add as 2 FLOPs, for a sequence of $n$ tokens. - **Dense part.** $Q,K,V$ and output projections: four matmuls of $n\\times d$ by $d\\times d$, so $4\\cdot2nd^2=8nd^2$. FFN: $2\\cdot2n\\,d\\,d_{ff}=16nd^2$ for $d_{ff}=4d$. Total $24\\,n\\,d^2$, which is *linear* in $n$. - **Attention scores.** $QK^{\\top}$ is $n\\times d\\ (\\text{over all heads})$ times $d\\times n$: $2n^2d$; multiplying the weights by $V$ costs another $2n^2d$. Total $4\\,n^2\\,d$, which is *quadratic* in $n$. The two are equal when $4n^2d=24nd^2$, that is at $$ n^{\\ast}=6\\,d . $$ {#eq:cross} For $d=768$ that is $n^{\\ast}=4608$ tokens; the test `test_flops_crossover_is_six_d` checks the algebra. ![FLOPs per layer against sequence length for $d=768$ (log-log). The dense part (blue) is a straight line of slope 1, the attention scores (orange) a line of slope 2. They cross at $n=6d=4608$. At $n=1024$ the scores are 18.2% of the layer; at $n=32768$ they are 87.7%.](figures/fig04_flops.png){width=75%} The numbers behind the figure: at $n=1024$, attention scores $3.2$ GFLOP versus dense $14.5$ GFLOP; at $n=4608$ both are $65.2$ GFLOP; at $n=32768$, $3299$ versus $464$ GFLOP. **Memory** is also quadratic: the weight matrix of one head at $n=32768$ in 16-bit numbers is $32768^2\\times2$ bytes $=2.1$ GB, which is why long contexts are expensive (Unit 10). Table 1 of Vaswani et al. makes the comparison with other layer types (their notation: $n$ length, $d$ width): | layer type | complexity per layer | sequential operations | maximum path length | |:--|:--|:--:|:--:| | self-attention | $O(n^2\\,d)$ | $O(1)$ | $O(1)$ | | recurrent | $O(n\\,d^2)$ | $O(n)$ | $O(n)$ | Table: two rows of the paper's Table 1. Attention is *more* expensive per layer when $n>d$ but needs $O(1)$ sequential steps (parallel over positions) and connects any two positions in one step. ### 3.4.2 Encoder, decoder, encoder-decoder | family | attention pattern | trained by | examples | good at | |:--|:--|:--|:--|:--| | encoder only | full (every token sees every token) | masked-token prediction | BERT | classification, search embeddings, tagging | | decoder only | causal mask | next-token prediction | GPT-style models | open-ended generation, chat | | encoder-decoder | encoder full; decoder causal plus cross-attention | text-to-text | original Transformer, T5 | translation, summarisation | Table: BERT (Devlin et al. 2018) is an encoder trained to fill in masked tokens; T5 (Raffel et al. 2019) casts every task as text in, text out, with an encoder and a decoder. The decoder-only form is the design behind most current chat models, which is why Unit 3's next-token view applies to them directly. The **parameter sizes of the original model.** Table 3 of Vaswani et al. gives the base model $N=6$ layers, $d=512$, $d_{ff}=2048$, $h=8$, $d_k=d_v=64$, with 65 million parameters. From the block count above, an encoder layer has $4d^2+2d\\,d_{ff}=3{,}145{,}728$ weights, a decoder layer one more attention ($+4d^2$) for $4{,}194{,}304$; six of each is $44{,}040{,}192$. Adding one shared embedding table of about $37{,}000\\times512=18{,}944{,}000$ (the paper's shared vocabulary is \"about 37000\" tokens) gives $62{,}984{,}192\\approx63$ million, within 3% of the paper's 65 million. I did not resolve the remaining 2 million (the exact vocabulary size in the authors' code, or components I have not counted). ## 3.5 Tier 5 (hardest): open questions 1. **Is the sinusoidal encoding still the right idea?** The paper found learned positional embeddings gave \"nearly identical results\" (Section 3.5), and chose sinusoids in the hope that they would extrapolate to longer sequences than any seen in training. Many current models instead rotate the queries and keys by position-dependent angles (rotary embeddings, Su et al. 2021), which keeps the property of @eq:pedot, that the score depends on the *offset*, but builds it into the attention score rather than adding it to the input. Which choice generalises best to very long contexts is still an active empirical question; I have not tested it here. 2. **Quadratic cost.** Figure 6 shows the scores dominate beyond $n=6d$. Whether exact attention can be replaced by something cheaper without losing quality at scale is open; it matters for every long-context model. 3. **What do heads actually do?** In a random block the heads differ only by their random weights. In a trained model it is an empirical finding, not a theorem, that some heads track simple patterns (the previous token, the same word earlier in the text). Interpretability research tests such claims head by head. # 4. Apply ## 4.1 Worked problems **Problem 1 (attention by hand).** Reproduce Section 3.2.2 with $Q=(0,1)$ instead. By symmetry the weights are $(0.330,\\,0.670)$ and the output $(3.302,\\,6.698)$. **Problem 2 (size of a model).** GPT-2 small has $L=12$ blocks of width $d=768$. The block weights are $12\\cdot12\\cdot768^2=84{,}934{,}656$, as in Unit 3. The crossover length for these blocks is $n^{\\ast}=6\\cdot768=4608$, so a context of 1024 tokens is still in the regime where the dense part (82% of the layer's FLOPs, Figure 6) dominates. **Problem 3 (relative position).** With $d=64$, what is $PE_{0}\\cdot PE_{10}$? From @eq:pedot it is $\\sum_{i=0}^{31}\\cos(10\\cdot10000^{-2i/64})$, and the code gives $21.0516$ (the value is the same for $PE_{50}\\cdot PE_{60}$: $21.0516$). **Problem 4 (memory of the score matrix).** One head, $n=1024$, 16-bit values: $1024^2\\times2\\approx2.1$ MB. With 12 heads, $25.2$ MB per layer per sequence, and the number grows 4 times each time the context doubles. ## 4.2 Code and figures Everything is in `codes/llm/transformer_np/` (see its README). The core is attention plus one block: ```python def attention(Q, K, V, mask=None): scores = Q @ K.T / np.sqrt(Q.shape[-1]) if mask is not None: scores = np.where(mask, scores, -1e30) # forbidden pairs -> weight 0 w = softmax(scores, axis=-1) return w @ V, w def block(x, P, mask=None, memory=None): # pre-LN: x + MHA(LN x), then x + FFN(LN x) a, w = mha(layer_norm(x, **P[\"ln1\"]), layer_norm(x, **P[\"ln1\"]), P[\"att\"], P[\"h\"], mask) x = x + a ... ``` All six figure scripts print the numbers quoted in this document. ::: {.keyidea title=\"Try it in the playground\"} The **Attention heatmap** widget lets you edit the sentence and pick which word is looking, with and without the causal mask. The **Positional encoding** widget shows the sinusoids and the dot-product-versus-offset curve for the width and number of positions you choose. ::: # 5. Analyze *Checklist items used: limits (5.1), dominance map (5.2), sensitivity (5.3), invariants (5.4), failure modes (5.5).* ## 5.1 Limits - $n=1$: the softmax has one entry, weight 1, so attention returns the value itself. - All scores equal (for example $Q=0$): uniform average, as in row \"The\" of Figure 1. - $d_k\\to\\infty$ without scaling: the softmax becomes an argmax (Figure 3). - $h=1$ versus $h=d$: the weight count is unchanged ($4d^2$), but $h=d$ would give width-1 heads whose scores are a product of two scalars, too little to express useful similarity. ## 5.2 Dominance map The dense term $24nd^2$ and the score term $4n^2d$ trade places at $n=6d$ (Figure 6). Below it, making the model wider is what costs; above it, making the context longer is. ## 5.3 Sensitivity The attention output is most sensitive to the scale of $Q$ and $K$. Doubling both scales the scores by 4 and sharpens the softmax sharply; this is the same mechanism as lowering the sampling temperature in Unit 3, now inside the model. ## 5.4 Invariants - *Permutation equivariance without PE.* Verified to $9\\times10^{-16}$ (Figure 5, test `test_permutation_equivariance_without_pe_and_order_with_pe`). - *No future leakage with a causal mask.* Changing tokens 5 and later does not change outputs 0 to 4; the test `test_causal_no_future_leak` checks it. - *Row sums.* Every attention row sums to 1 (`test_attention_rows_sum_to_one_and_mask`). ## 5.5 Failure modes - **Long-context dilution.** With $n$ keys a head must spread weight over many positions; a relevant token can be drowned by many weakly matching ones. - **Quadratic memory** for the score matrix (Problem 4). - **Position extrapolation.** A model trained on lengths up to $n_{\\max}$ may behave badly beyond it, whichever encoding is used. - **Masking mistakes.** Forgetting the causal mask in a decoder leaks the answer during training, and the loss falls suspiciously fast. # 6. Evaluate **Transformer versus recurrent network.** The Transformer wins on parallelism (all positions at once on a GPU), path length (1 versus $n$), and in practice on quality at scale. The recurrent network wins on memory at inference: it stores a fixed-size state, while a Transformer must keep all past keys and values (Unit 10). Both facts are in Table 1 of the paper in different words. **Inductive bias.** A convolution assumes locality; an RNN assumes order and a running summary; self-attention assumes almost nothing except that content-based lookup is useful. Less built-in structure means more data is needed, and more flexibility once it is available. That trade-off is the quiet reason scale (Unit 3) mattered so much for Transformers. **When not to use full attention.** Very long sequences (hundreds of thousands of tokens) with a tight memory budget; small data sets where a model with a stronger bias, such as a convolution for images of a few hundred samples, will do better. **Wider context.** Attention is a differentiable form of *associative memory*: a soft dictionary lookup. That framing connects the Transformer to older ideas about content-addressable memory, and to retrieval in Unit 5, where the \"keys\" are documents. # 7. Create ::: {.create title=\"What you can build from this\"} 1. **Attention visualiser:** feed any sentence through `block` with random weights and plot the heads; then replace the weights with hand-built ones, as in Figure 1, to make a given word attend to another. 2. **Order test:** show that bag-of-words ($PE=0$) cannot separate \"dog bites man\" from \"man bites dog\" and that adding `sinusoidal_pe` can. 3. **Cost calculator:** a function that returns the crossover length and the FLOP split for any $(d,\\,d_{ff},\\,n)$, and plot it for $d=512,\\,768,\\,4096$. 4. **Combine with Unit 3:** put `block` under the `sample` function from session 001 to build a (random-weight) text generator with the right *interfaces*; training it is the next step. 5. **Module for the open-source package:** add rotary embeddings to `transformer_np` with a test that the score depends only on the offset. ::: # 8. Visualization lesson | Chart type | Best for | How it misleads | matplotlib call | |:--|:--|:--|:--| | heatmap (`imshow`) | a matrix of weights (Fig. 1, 4 left) | colour scale hides small values; a sequential map implies order | `ax.imshow(M, cmap=\"Blues\", vmin=0, vmax=1)` | | diverging heatmap | values with a meaningful zero (PE matrix) | the midpoint must be 0 or the colours lie | `cmap=\"RdBu_r\", vmin=-1, vmax=1` | | line on semilog axis | quantities spanning many orders (Fig. 2) | straight lines look like exponentials only if the data are | `ax.semilogy(x, y)` | | log-log line | power laws; slopes are exponents (Fig. 6) | only slopes are readable, not differences | `ax.loglog(x, y)` | | strip plot with medians | a few repeats of an experiment (Fig. 5) | hides the distribution if points overlap | `ax.semilogy([i]*len(v), v, \"o\")` | Table: charts taught here; log them in the visualization log. How to read the log-log plot: a line of slope 1 means cost $\\propto n$, slope 2 means $\\propto n^2$; where two lines cross, the dominance changes. # 9. Problem set ::: {.exercise title=\"Graded exercises\"} 1. (easy) With $Q=(0,\\,1)$, $K=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$, $V=\\begin{pmatrix}10&0\\\\0&10\\end{pmatrix}$ compute the attention output. 2. (medium) A model has $d=1024$, $d_{ff}=4096$. Compute the parameters of one block (weights only) and the sequence length $n^{\\ast}$ at which attention scores equal the dense FLOPs. 3. (medium-hard) Prove $PE_{pos}\\!\\cdot\\!PE_{pos+k}=\\sum_i\\cos(\\omega_ik)$ from the definition, and show that the value at $k=0$ is $d/2$. 4. (hard) Show that attention without positional encoding is permutation-equivariant: if $P$ is a permutation matrix, $\\mathrm{Attention}(PQ,PK,PV)=P\\,\\mathrm{Attention}(Q,K,V)$. (Hint: the softmax acts on each row, and $PQK^{\\top}P^{\\top}$ is a re-indexing of $QK^{\\top}$.) 5. (hardest) For a decoder-only model with a causal mask, show that the attention-score FLOPs per layer over a full sequence are about *half* of $4n^2d$ if the masked entries are not computed. Why do many implementations still compute them? ::: # 10. Retrieval practice and recap **Answers to the Section 2 quiz.** 1. $QK^{\\top}$ is $n_q\\times n_k$; the output is $n_q\\times d_v$. 2. Raw dot-product scores have variance $d_k$; dividing by $\\sqrt{d_k}$ keeps the variance at 1 so the softmax does not saturate (Section 3.3.1). 3. Nothing: the outputs are permuted exactly like the inputs, so the model cannot see order (Figure 5). 4. Attention from a position to any *later* position. 5. T5 (and the original Transformer): the encoder-decoder models. BERT is encoder only; GPT-style models are decoder only and have no cross-attention. **Explain it to a friend.** - Why can a Transformer connect \"it\" to \"animal\" in one step when an RNN needs many? - What would break if you removed the positional encoding? - Why does making the context 4 times longer make attention 16 times more expensive in the score computation? **Re-derive tomorrow without looking:** (a) $\\mathrm{Var}(q^\\top k)=d_k$; (b) $PE_{pos}\\!\\cdot\\!PE_{pos+k}=\\sum_i\\cos\\omega_ik$; (c) $12d^2$ parameters per block and the crossover $n^{\\ast}=6d$. # 11. Where next - **Next node:** Unit 1 (the wider map of generative models: autoencoders, VAEs, GANs, diffusion), then Units 4 to 10, which all build on the block you have just assembled. - **Terms met but not taught:** rotary embedding, FlashAttention, grouped-query attention, KV cache (Unit 10). - **Open threads:** the 2 million parameters between the paper's 65M and the 63M count in Section 3.4.2; which positional scheme extrapolates best (Section 3.5).", "u3": "# Session map ::: {.keyidea title=\"Outcome of this session\"} After this session you can: 1. explain an LLM as one repeated operation, *predict a probability distribution over the next token*, and compute its training loss and perplexity by hand; 2. train a byte-pair-encoding (BPE) tokenizer on any text and say why subwords beat words and characters; 3. turn logits into a token with greedy, temperature, top-$k$, top-$p$ and beam decoding, and predict what each knob does to the distribution; 4. estimate the parameter count, training FLOPs and compute-optimal data size of a model from its shape; 5. run, read and modify the code in `codes/llm/tokens_decoding/`, and reproduce all five figures. ::: - **Prerequisites:** Python and NumPy; probability (distributions, expectation, logarithms); a first neural-network course (a layer is a matrix times a vector, followed by a nonlinearity). Transformers are only *used* here as a black box that outputs logits; the box is opened in Unit 2. - **Difficulty ladder used:** easy \u2192 medium \u2192 medium-hard \u2192 hard (Sections 3.1 to 3.4). The hardest tier is left as an open question in Section 3.5. - **Deliverables:** this document (.tex, .pdf, .docx), 5 figures, the module `codes/llm/tokens_decoding/` (BPE, decoding, scaling law; 6 tests), and the interactive widgets *BPE stepper* and *Next-token sampler* in the LLM Studio playground. - **Source anchors:** Sennrich, Haddow and Birch 2016, arXiv:1508.07909 (BPE for subword units); Holtzman et al. 2019, arXiv:1904.09751 (nucleus / top-$p$ sampling); Hoffmann et al. 2022, arXiv:2203.15556 (compute-optimal training; the constants in Eq. (10) of that paper are the ones used in Section 5). All three IDs were checked against arXiv on the day of writing. # 1. Terms: the term tree Everything an LLM does rests on a short list of words. Each one is defined in Section 2 and used in Sections 3 and 4. | Term | Symbol | One-line meaning | Where it appears | New / known | |:-----|:------:|:-----------------|:-----------------|:-----------:| | token | $x_i$ | the unit of text the model reads and writes (a word piece) | everywhere | new | | vocabulary | $V$ | the fixed set of tokens; its size is also written $V$ | 3.2 | new | | next-token prediction | $P(x_{n+1}\\mid x_{1:n})$ | the single task an LLM is trained on | 3.2 | new | | cross-entropy loss | $\\mathcal{L}$ | average of $-\\ln$ (probability given to the true next token) | 3.2 | new | | perplexity | $\\mathrm{PPL}$ | $e^{\\mathcal{L}}$: the effective number of equally likely choices | 3.2 | new | | byte-pair encoding (BPE) | | repeatedly merge the most frequent adjacent pair of symbols | 3.3 | new | | embedding | $E$ | a learned vector for every token id | 3.3.2 | new | | logits | $z$ | the model's raw scores, one per vocabulary entry | 3.4.1 | new | | softmax | $p_i$ | converts scores to a probability distribution | 3.4.1 | new | | temperature | $T$ | divides the logits before softmax; sharpens or flattens | 3.4.1 | new | | greedy / beam decoding | | pick the best token / keep the best $B$ partial sequences | 3.4.2 | new | | top-$k$ / top-$p$ | $k,\\ p$ | sample only from the $k$ best / the smallest set with mass $\\ge p$ | 3.4.2 | new | | scaling law | $L(N,D)$ | loss as a function of parameters $N$ and tokens $D$ | 3.4.3 | new | | context window | $n_{\\max}$ | the number of tokens the model can read at once | 3.2 | new | Terms *used but not taught here* (they are registered as later nodes): attention and the Transformer block (Unit 2), KV cache (Unit 10), instruction tuning (Unit 6). # 2. Remember ::: {.definition title=\"Token and vocabulary\"} A **token** is a string chunk such as `low`, `est` or ` the`. The **vocabulary** $\\mathcal{V}$ is a fixed list of $V$ tokens, each with an integer **id**. A text is turned into a list of ids by a **tokenizer**; the model only ever sees ids. Typical $V$ for modern models is $3\\times10^{4}$ to $2.5\\times10^{5}$. ::: ::: {.definition title=\"Language model\"} A language model assigns a probability to every token sequence. By the chain rule of probability, $$ P(x_{1:n}) = \\prod_{i=1}^{n} P(x_i \\mid x_{1:i-1}), $$ {#eq:chain} so it is enough to learn one function: *given the prefix, output a distribution over the next token*. That function is **next-token prediction**. ::: ::: {.definition title=\"Logits, softmax, temperature\"} The network outputs one real number per vocabulary entry, the **logits** $z\\in\\mathbb{R}^{V}$. The **softmax with temperature $T>0$** turns them into probabilities: $$ p_i(T) = \\frac{e^{z_i/T}}{\\sum_{j=1}^{V} e^{z_j/T}}. $$ {#eq:softmax} $T=1$ is the model's own distribution. $T<1$ sharpens it, $T>1$ flattens it. ::: ::: {.definition title=\"Cross-entropy and perplexity\"} For a training text with true next tokens $x_{i}$, the **cross-entropy loss** is the mean negative log-probability the model gave to the truth, and the **perplexity** is its exponential: $$ \\mathcal{L} = -\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\ln p_\\theta(x_i\\mid x_{1:i-1}), \\qquad \\mathrm{PPL}=e^{\\mathcal{L}}. $$ {#eq:ce} ::: **Memory hooks.** - *Token = syllable-ish.* Not letters, not words; the pieces that occur often enough to deserve their own id. - *Logits are votes; softmax is the election; temperature is how sure the voters are.* Low $T$ is a landslide, high $T$ is a coin toss. - *Perplexity = the model's effective branching factor.* PPL = 10 means \"as unsure as a fair 10-sided die\". **Recall quiz** (answers in Section 10). 1. Write the chain rule that lets a model with one output head define a probability for a whole sentence. 2. What does $T\\to 0$ do to the sampled token? What does $T\\to\\infty$ do? 3. A model gives the true next token probability $0.25$ at every step. What is its perplexity? 4. In BPE, which pair is merged at each step? 5. Does top-$p$ keep a fixed number of tokens? Does top-$k$? # 3. Understand: the derivation ## 3.1 Tier 1 (easy): intuition and a picture Imagine a phone keyboard that suggests the next word. An LLM is that suggestion bar, scaled up until the suggestions are paragraphs. Two ideas do all the work. 1. **Text is cut into tokens, and each token becomes a vector.** The model never sees letters; it sees ids, and each id is looked up in a table of learned vectors (the embedding table). 2. **The model returns a probability for every possible next token, and a separate rule (decoding) chooses one.** The model is a *calculator of odds*; the decoding rule is the *decision*. The same model can sound careful or creative depending only on that rule. Writing a long answer is then a loop: predict, choose, append the chosen token to the prefix, predict again. This is why an LLM answer appears one token at a time, and why a bad early choice cannot be taken back. ![Why subwords: as BPE learns more merges, the same text needs fewer tokens (blue, left axis) while the vocabulary grows (orange, right axis). Zero merges is character-level: 1 token per character.](figures/fig01_bpe.png){width=80%} Figure 1 is the whole motivation for tokenization. Character-level text is short in vocabulary (27 symbols here) but long in sequence length; whole-word text is short in sequence length but has an unbounded vocabulary and cannot spell a word it has never seen. BPE sits in between and lets you choose where. ## 3.2 Tier 2 (medium): the definitions made precise **The model.** Write the whole network as a function $f_\\theta$ that maps a prefix $x_{1:i-1}$ (at most $n_{\\max}$ tokens, the **context window**) to logits $z\\in\\mathbb{R}^{V}$. Then $$ p_\\theta(x_i = v\\mid x_{1:i-1}) = \\mathrm{softmax}\\big(f_\\theta(x_{1:i-1})\\big)_v . $$ **Training.** Take a large text, cut it into sequences of tokens, and for each position make the model predict the true next token. Minimise @eq:ce with gradient descent. There are no human labels: the text *is* the label (this is called self-supervised learning). Because the sum runs over every position of every sequence, one sequence of length $n$ supplies $n$ training examples. **Worked example: loss and perplexity.** Suppose on a four-token sentence the model gave the true tokens probabilities $0.5,\\ 0.25,\\ 0.125,\\ 0.5$. Then $$ \\begin{aligned} \\mathcal{L} &= \\tfrac14\\big(\\ln 2+\\ln 4+\\ln 8+\\ln 2\\big) = \\tfrac14\\ln 128 = 1.2128,\\\\ \\mathrm{PPL} &= e^{\\mathcal{L}} = 128^{1/4} = 3.364 . \\end{aligned} $$ The perplexity is the geometric mean of $1/p$: the model was, on average, as unsure as a fair die with about $3.4$ faces. A model that guesses uniformly over a vocabulary of $V=50{,}000$ has $\\mathrm{PPL}=50{,}000$; every real improvement is a reduction from that number. ::: {.keyidea title=\"What the loss number means\"} Loss $\\mathcal{L}$ is in **nats** (natural log). Dividing by $\\ln 2$ gives **bits per token**. Perplexity $=e^{\\mathcal{L}}$. A drop of $0.1$ nats is a $10\\%$ cut in perplexity, and it is a large improvement in practice. ::: ## 3.3 Tier 3 (medium-hard): the main derivations ### 3.3.1 Byte-pair encoding, step by step BPE (Sennrich et al. 2016) starts from characters and learns a merge table from data. **Algorithm.** 1. Split every word into characters and append an end-of-word marker `</w>`. Count how often each word occurs. 2. Count every adjacent pair of symbols, weighted by word frequency. 3. Merge the most frequent pair into a single new symbol everywhere. Record the merge. 4. Repeat steps 2-3 for $M$ merges. $M$ is the one hyperparameter; final vocabulary $\\approx$ (initial characters) $+\\,M$. To encode new text, split into characters and replay the recorded merges **in the order they were learned**. **Worked example** (this is exactly what `train_bpe` prints for the toy corpus `low`$\\times5$, `lower`$\\times2$, `newest`$\\times6$, `widest`$\\times3$): | step | merged pair | count | new symbol | |:--:|:--|--:|:--| | 1 | `t` + `</w>` | 9 | `t</w>` | | 2 | `s` + `t</w>` | 9 | `st</w>` | | 3 | `e` + `st</w>` | 9 | `est</w>` | | 4 | `o` + `w` | 7 | `ow` | | 5 | `l` + `ow` | 7 | `low` | | 6 | `w` + `est</w>` | 6 | `west</w>` | Table: first six merges. Step 1 counts `t</w>` as 6 (newest) + 3 (widest) = 9. The suffix `est` emerges *by itself* from frequency, with no linguistic rule. Encoding the unseen word `lowest` with these merges gives `low` + `est</w>`: a word that never occurred in training is spelled from pieces that did. That is why modern LLMs never meet an \"unknown word\". (Byte-level BPE, used by GPT-style models, starts from the 256 byte values instead of characters, so *any* string is representable.) ::: {.warning title=\"Common mistake\"} BPE merges are **ordered**. Applying them in a different order, or greedily by longest match, gives different tokens from the training tokenizer. The same text then lands on token ids the model never saw in that form. ::: ### 3.3.2 Embeddings A token id $v$ selects row $v$ of a learned matrix $E\\in\\mathbb{R}^{V\\times d}$: $e_v=E_{v,:}$. Equivalently, multiply the one-hot vector $\\mathbf{1}_v\\in\\mathbb{R}^{V}$ by $E$: $$ e_v = \\mathbf{1}_v^{\\top}E . $$ The $d$ numbers in $e_v$ are free parameters learned by gradient descent, with no meaning at the start. After training, tokens used in similar contexts have vectors pointing in similar directions, and the usual measure of \"similar direction\" is the **cosine similarity** $$ \\cos(u,v)=\\frac{u^{\\top}v}{\\lVert u\\rVert\\,\\lVert v\\rVert}\\in[-1,1]. $$ {#eq:cos} ![Toy two-dimensional embeddings (built by hand to show the idea, not learned). king and queen point the same way (cosine 0.906), apple and pear almost the same (0.981), king and apple are unrelated (-0.211). The red cross is king - man + woman, which lands exactly on queen.](figures/fig05_embeddings.png){width=60%} The figure is a *constructed* example: I placed the vectors so that the king-queen offset equals the man-woman offset, so the analogy is exact by design (error $2\\times10^{-16}$). In real models the analogy holds approximately and only for some relations; do not treat it as a law. ### 3.3.3 Gradient of the loss with respect to the logits This one result explains why training is stable and simple. For one position with true token $y$ and logits $z$ (take $T=1$), the loss is $\\ell=-\\ln p_y$ with $p_i=e^{z_i}/S$, $S=\\sum_j e^{z_j}$. $$ \\begin{aligned} \\ell &= -z_y+\\ln S &&\\text{(since } \\ln p_y = z_y-\\ln S)\\\\ \\frac{\\partial \\ell}{\\partial z_i} &= -\\delta_{iy}+\\frac{1}{S}\\frac{\\partial S}{\\partial z_i} &&\\text{(derivative of }\\ln S)\\\\ &= -\\delta_{iy}+\\frac{e^{z_i}}{S} &&\\text{(only the term } j=i \\text{ depends on } z_i)\\\\ &= p_i-\\delta_{iy}. \\end{aligned} $$ {#eq:grad} In vector form the gradient is simply $p-\\mathbf{1}_y$: **predicted distribution minus the one-hot truth**. It pushes the true logit up by $1-p_y$ and every other logit down by $p_i$. It is bounded by 1 in each component, so a confidently wrong prediction cannot produce an exploding gradient at the output. ## 3.4 Tier 4 (hard): decoding, the other half of the model The network has produced logits $z$. *Decoding* is the rule that turns them into the next token. Each strategy below is a different answer to one tension: **quality** (pick likely tokens) against **diversity** (do not repeat yourself). ### 3.4.1 Temperature Let $p_i(T)$ be as in @eq:softmax and let $i^\\ast=\\arg\\max_i z_i$ be the unique largest logit. Divide numerator and denominator by $e^{z_{i^\\ast}/T}$: $$ p_i(T)=\\frac{e^{(z_i-z_{i^\\ast})/T}}{1+\\sum_{j\\neq i^\\ast}e^{(z_j-z_{i^\\ast})/T}} . $$ Every exponent $(z_j-z_{i^\\ast})/T$ with $j\\ne i^\\ast$ is negative. As $T\\to0^{+}$ it tends to $-\\infty$, so $p_{i^\\ast}\\to1$ and all others $\\to0$: **greedy**. As $T\\to\\infty$ every exponent tends to $0$, so $p_i\\to 1/V$: **uniform**. In between, $T$ rescales the *gaps* between logits: doubling $T$ halves every gap. ![Left: the same eight logits at three temperatures. Right: entropy (in bits) against T; it rises from 0 toward the uniform value of 3 bits.](figures/fig02_temperature.png){width=90%} For these eight logits the numbers (from `fig02_temperature.py`) are: | $T$ | $p(\\text{the})$ | entropy (bits) | |:--:|--:|--:| | 0.3 | 0.922 | 0.465 | | 1.0 | 0.467 | 2.214 | | 2.0 | 0.282 | 2.751 | Table: lowering $T$ from 1 to 0.3 nearly doubles the chance of the top token and cuts the entropy by a factor of almost five. ### 3.4.2 Greedy, beam, top-$k$ and top-$p$ **Greedy** takes $\\arg\\max_i p_i$ every step. It is fast and deterministic, but it is *myopic*: the best token now can lead to a worse sentence. **Beam search** keeps the $B$ highest-scoring partial sequences, where a sequence's score is the sum of its token log-probabilities $\\sum_i\\ln p_i$, and extends all of them each step. **Worked example: greedy loses.** Token 0 is the start; the next-token table is $$ P=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0.6&0.4\\\\0.5&0.25&0.25\\\\0.01&0.01&0.98\\end{pmatrix},\\quad \\text{row }=\\text{previous token}. $$ Greedy for two steps picks token 1 (0.6) and then token 0 (0.5): probability $0.6\\times0.5=0.300$ ($\\ln=-1.204$). The path $0\\to2\\to2$ has probability $0.4\\times0.98=0.392$ ($\\ln=-0.936$). Beam search with $B=3$ finds it; greedy cannot, because it commits to the 0.6 branch. The test `test_beam_beats_greedy_on_trap` checks this. ::: {.warning title=\"Beam search is not the best sampler for open-ended text\"} Holtzman et al. (2019) showed that maximising likelihood on open-ended generation produces bland, repetitive text, which is why chat models usually *sample* rather than use beam search. Beam search stays the standard for tasks with one right answer, such as translation or code. ::: **Top-$k$.** Keep the $k$ most probable tokens, set the rest to zero, renormalise, then sample. **Top-$p$ (nucleus).** Sort tokens by probability and keep the smallest prefix whose cumulative mass is at least $p$: $$ \\mathcal{V}_p=\\text{the smallest set } S \\text{ with } \\sum_{i\\in S}p_i\\ \\ge\\ p,\\qquad q_i=\\frac{p_i\\,\\mathbf{1}[i\\in\\mathcal{V}_p]}{\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal{V}_p}p_j}. $$ {#eq:topp} Worked example: $p=(0.5,\\,0.3,\\,0.15,\\,0.05)$ and $p_{\\text{top}}=0.8$. The first two tokens carry $0.5+0.3=0.8\\ge0.8$, so the nucleus has size 2 and $q=(0.625,\\,0.375,\\,0,\\,0)$. For top-$k=2$ the same vector is kept, but the *rule* is different: ![Cumulative probability against token rank. When the model is confident (left) top-p = 0.9 keeps 1 token; when it is unsure (right) it keeps 25. Top-k = 10 keeps 10 in both cases.](figures/fig03_topk_topp.png){width=90%} ::: {.keyidea title=\"Why top-p is adaptive\"} Top-$k$ keeps a **fixed number** of tokens, so it can be too loose when the model is sure and too tight when it is not. Top-$p$ keeps a **fixed amount of probability**, so the number of tokens adapts to the model's confidence (1 versus 25 in Figure 3). ::: **The standard order.** Temperature is applied to logits first, then the top-$k$ and top-$p$ filters, then sampling. Changing the order changes the answer, because the filters work on probabilities that already depend on $T$. ### 3.4.3 How big, and how much data **Counting parameters.** A decoder-only Transformer with $L$ blocks of width $d$ has, per block, $4d^{2}$ attention weights (query, key, value and output matrices, each $d\\times d$) and $8d^{2}$ in the feed-forward layer ($d\\to4d\\to d$). Ignoring small terms (biases, layer norms), plus the token embedding table, $$ N\\;\\approx\\;12\\,L\\,d^{2}+V\\,d . $$ {#eq:params} For the smallest GPT-2 ($L=12$, $d=768$, $V=50{,}257$): $12\\cdot12\\cdot768^{2}=84{,}934{,}656$ and $50{,}257\\cdot768=38{,}597{,}376$, a total of $123{,}532{,}032\\approx1.24\\times10^{8}$, consistent with the commonly quoted 124M. (I derived this from the formula; I did not download the model to count its tensors.) **Counting training compute.** One forward pass costs about one multiply and one add per weight per token, so $2N$ FLOPs per token. The backward pass costs about twice the forward pass. Therefore $$ C\\;\\approx\\;(2N+4N)\\,D = 6\\,N\\,D\\quad\\text{FLOPs}, $$ {#eq:flops} where $D$ is the number of training tokens. This approximation ignores the attention score computation, which matters only for very long contexts. **The scaling law.** Hoffmann et al. (2022) fit the final loss as $$ L(N,D)=E+\\frac{A}{N^{\\alpha}}+\\frac{B}{D^{\\beta}},\\qquad E=1.69,\\ A=406.4,\\ B=410.7,\\ \\alpha=0.34,\\ \\beta=0.28 $$ {#eq:chin} (their Eq. (10), values checked against the paper). Read it term by term: $E$ is the *irreducible* loss (the entropy of text itself), $A/N^{\\alpha}$ is the penalty for a model that is too small, and $B/D^{\\beta}$ is the penalty for too little data. For a fixed budget $C=6ND$, setting $D=C/(6N)$ turns $L$ into a function of $N$ alone, with a minimum where the two penalties balance. ![IsoFLOP curves from Eq. (10) with D = C/(6N). Each curve has one best model size (filled dot). The diamonds mark the rule of thumb D = 20 N; the two differ by under 0.01 nats because the minimum is flat.](figures/fig04_scaling.png){width=80%} ## 3.5 Tier 5 (hardest): an open question Figure 4 contains a result that surprised me while writing it, so I report it as it is. Minimising Eq. (10) at each budget gives these data-to-parameter ratios (from `fig04_scaling.py`): | budget $C$ (FLOPs) | best $N$ | best $D$ | $D/N$ | |:--:|--:|--:|--:| | $10^{21}$ | $1.8\\times10^{9}$ | $9.2\\times10^{10}$ | 51 | | $10^{22}$ | $5.2\\times10^{9}$ | $3.2\\times10^{11}$ | 62 | | $10^{23}$ | $1.5\\times10^{10}$ | $1.1\\times10^{12}$ | 76 | | $10^{24}$ | $4.1\\times10^{10}$ | $4.1\\times10^{12}$ | 100 | Table: the minimum of the fitted formula does **not** give $D/N=20$, and the ratio grows with $C$. This is not a bug. The paper itself reports that this parametric fit (its \"approach 3\") gives $N_{\\text{opt}}\\propto C^{0.46}$ and $D_{\\text{opt}}\\propto C^{0.54}$, so $D/N\\propto C^{0.08}$ rises slowly, while its other two approaches give exponents near $0.50$, i.e. a constant ratio, which is where the famous \"about 20 tokens per parameter\" comes from. Which exponent is right, and how much of the disagreement is noise in the fit, is an open empirical question; I have not resolved it here. **For planning, the rule of thumb and the fit agree on loss to within 0.01 nats (Figure 4), so the choice matters little for the loss but a lot for how large a model you must serve.** # 4. Apply ## 4.1 Worked problems **Problem 1 (tokens and cost).** A tokenizer averages about $0.3$ tokens per character on technical English (Figure 1 shows $0.317$ after 320 merges on this course's syllabus). How many tokens is a 6{,}942-character page? Answer: $6{,}942\\times0.317\\approx2{,}200$ tokens (the run gives $2{,}204$). A 4{,}096-token context window therefore holds about two such pages. **Problem 2 (size and compute of GPT-2 small).** From @eq:params, $N\\approx1.24\\times10^{8}$. Using $D=20N=2.5\\times10^{9}$ tokens, $C=6ND\\approx1.8\\times10^{18}$ FLOPs. A GPU sustaining $10^{14}$ FLOP/s would need about $1.8\\times10^{4}$ s, i.e. 5 hours, at perfect efficiency; real utilisation is far lower. **Problem 3 (temperature by hand).** Logits $z=(2,1,0)$. At $T=1$, $p\\propto(e^{2},e^{1},e^{0})=(7.389,\\,2.718,\\,1)$ with sum $11.107$, so $p=(0.665,\\,0.245,\\,0.090)$. At $T=0.5$, $p\\propto(e^{4},e^{2},1)=(54.6,\\,7.389,\\,1)$, sum $62.99$, so $p=(0.867,\\,0.117,\\,0.016)$: the top token rose from $0.665$ to $0.867$. ## 4.2 Code and figures Everything is in `codes/llm/tokens_decoding/` (see its README). The core of decoding is four lines: ```python def softmax(z, T=1.0): z = np.asarray(z, dtype=float) / T z = z - z.max() # subtract max: same answer, no overflow e = np.exp(z) return e / e.sum() def sample(logits, T=1.0, top_k=None, top_p=None, rng=None): p = softmax(logits, T) # 1. temperature if top_k is not None: p = top_k_filter(p, top_k) # 2. keep k best if top_p is not None: p = top_p_filter(p, top_p) # 3. keep the nucleus return int(rng.choice(len(p), p=p)) # 4. draw one token ``` The BPE trainer is `train_bpe(text, num_merges)`; it returns the ordered merge list and a history of (pair, count, vocabulary size). To reproduce Figure 1 run `figures/fig01_bpe.py`, which trains on the Syllabus text with 0 to 320 merges. All five figure scripts print the numbers quoted in this document. ::: {.keyidea title=\"Try it in the playground\"} Open the **BPE stepper** and press *Merge* to watch the table above appear one row at a time. Open the **Next-token sampler** and drag temperature, top-$k$ and top-$p$ while watching which bars survive. Both are linked from the Unit 3 page of the site. ::: # 5. Analyze *Checklist items used: limits (5.1), dominance (5.2), order-of-magnitude estimate (5.3), failure modes (5.4).* ## 5.1 Limits - $T\\to0$: greedy (Section 3.4.1). $T\\to\\infty$: uniform over all $V$ tokens, i.e. garbage. Useful range is roughly $0.2\\le T\\le1.5$. - $k=1$ or $p\\to0$: greedy. $k=V$ or $p=1$: no filtering, only temperature remains. - Merges $M\\to0$: character-level. $M\\to\\infty$: every training word is one token. Figure 1 shows the sequence length falling and the vocabulary rising smoothly between the two. ## 5.2 Which term dominates? In @eq:params, $12Ld^{2}$ exceeds the embedding term $Vd$ when $12Ld>V$. For GPT-2 small, $12\\cdot12\\cdot768=110{,}592>50{,}257$ so the blocks dominate (69%), but the embedding table is still $31\\%$ of the parameters. For a tiny model it can be most of them. ## 5.3 Order-of-magnitude estimate At $N=7\\times10^{10}$ and $D=1.4\\times10^{12}$ (the size the Chinchilla paper trains), $C=6ND\\approx5.9\\times10^{23}$ FLOPs. The code `compute_optimal(5.76e23)` returns $N=6.9\\times10^{10}$ and $D=1.39\\times10^{12}$ under the $D=20N$ rule, matching that scale (the test `test_compute_optimal_matches_chinchilla_scale` checks it). ## 5.4 Failure modes - **Spelling and arithmetic.** A model sees `strawberry` as a few subword tokens, not letters, so letter counting is genuinely hard for it. This is a tokenizer property, not stupidity. - **Whitespace and language bias.** A tokenizer trained mostly on English splits other languages into many more tokens, which makes them slower and more expensive for the same content. - **Repetition loops under greedy or low-$T$ decoding.** Once a phrase is likely, repeating it is the most likely continuation, and nothing breaks the cycle. - **Numerical overflow.** Exponentiating large logits overflows; subtracting the maximum (as in the code) leaves the result unchanged and avoids it. # 6. Evaluate **Tokenization choices.** Characters: tiny vocabulary, long sequences, slow; words: short sequences, huge vocabulary, out-of-vocabulary failures; BPE/SentencePiece/byte-level: the pragmatic middle, and now universal. The cost is the oddities in 5.4. **Decoding choices.** | Task | Good choice | Why | |:--|:--|:--| | translation, code, extraction | greedy or beam, low $T$ | one right answer, want consistency | | chat, brainstorming | $T\\approx0.7$-$1.0$, top-$p\\approx0.9$ | variety without nonsense | | creative writing | higher $T$, top-$p$ | explore the tail | Table: rules of thumb, not laws. Always test on your own task. **When *not* to trust these numbers.** The scaling constants are fitted to one family of models and one data distribution; they are not constants of nature. Do not use Eq. (10) to predict a model with a different architecture, data mix or training recipe. A lower loss does not guarantee better usefulness: Unit 9 treats evaluation. **Wider context.** A language model is a very large compression machine: the loss in bits per token is the code length needed to store the text, so \"predicting the next word well\" and \"compressing text well\" are the same problem (Shannon, 1948). Whether prediction alone gives understanding is a live philosophical debate that this course does not settle. # 7. Create ::: {.create title=\"What you can build from this\"} 1. **Train your own tokenizer** on a folder of your own notes with `train_bpe`; plot tokens per character against merges for *your* text and compare with Figure 1. 2. **A temperature explorer** that prints ten samples at five temperatures from a small bigram model built from any text file. 3. **A beam-versus-greedy trap finder:** generate random $3\\times3$ tables and count how often beam search ($B=3$) beats greedy. 4. **Combine with Unit 2:** replace the bigram table with the self-attention model you build there, and keep the same `sample` function. 5. **Module for the open-source package:** extend `tokens_decoding` with repetition penalty and min-$p$ filtering, with tests. ::: # 8. Visualization lesson | Chart type | Best for | How it misleads | matplotlib call | |:--|:--|:--|:--| | dual-axis line | two quantities that share an x (Fig. 1) | the two scales can be stretched to suggest a relationship | `ax.twinx()` | | grouped bar | the same categories under three settings (Fig. 2 left) | colours too similar, or a truncated y-axis | `ax.bar(x+offset, h, width)` | | semilog line | quantities spanning orders of magnitude (Fig. 2 right, Fig. 4) | straight lines suggest power laws that may not exist | `ax.semilogx(x, y)` | | cumulative curve | \"how many items cover $p$ of the mass\" (Fig. 3) | a steep start hides the long tail | `ax.plot(rank, np.cumsum(p))` | | vector (arrow) plot | embeddings and directions (Fig. 5) | two dimensions cannot show the geometry of 4{,}096 | `ax.annotate(\"\", xy, xytext, arrowprops=...)` | Table: charts taught here. How to read each: in the cumulative curve, find where it crosses your threshold and read the rank on the x-axis; in the IsoFLOP plot, the minimum of each curve is the best model size for that budget. # 9. Problem set ::: {.exercise title=\"Graded exercises\"} 1. (easy) Compute perplexity for a model that assigns probabilities $0.1, 0.1, 0.1$ to the true tokens of a three-token text. 2. (medium) With logits $(3,1,0,-1)$, compute $p$ at $T=1$ and at $T=2$, and the top-$p=0.9$ nucleus at $T=1$. 3. (medium-hard) Run `train_bpe` on a paragraph of your choice for 30 merges. List the first five merges and explain each in terms of frequency. 4. (hard) Prove that for any logits the entropy of $\\mathrm{softmax}(z/T)$ is non-decreasing in $T$. (Hint: differentiate the entropy with respect to $1/T$.) 5. (hardest) Use Eq. (10) to derive $N_{\\text{opt}}(C)$ and show analytically that $N_{\\text{opt}}\\propto C^{\\beta/(\\alpha+\\beta)}$. Compare with the exponent $0.46$ reported in the paper, and explain why $D/N$ is not constant. ::: # 10. Retrieval practice and recap **Answers to the Section 2 quiz.** 1. $P(x_{1:n})=\\prod_i P(x_i\\mid x_{1:i-1})$, Eq. (1). 2. $T\\to0$: the top token gets all the probability (greedy). $T\\to\\infty$: uniform. 3. $\\mathcal{L}=\\ln4$, so $\\mathrm{PPL}=4$. 4. The most frequent adjacent pair, counted over the whole corpus, weighted by word frequency. 5. Top-$k$ keeps exactly $k$; top-$p$ keeps a variable number set by cumulative mass. **Explain it to a friend.** - Why can a chatbot give two different answers to the same question? - Why does an LLM sometimes miscount the letters in a word? - What is the difference between \"the model\" and \"the decoding strategy\"? **Re-derive tomorrow without looking:** (a) the gradient $p-\\mathbf{1}_y$ (@eq:grad); (b) the $T\\to0$ and $T\\to\\infty$ limits of the softmax; (c) $N\\approx12Ld^2+Vd$ and $C\\approx6ND$. # 11. Where next - **Next node:** Unit 2 (Transformer architecture), which opens the black box $f_\\theta$ used throughout this session; then Unit 4 (prompting) uses the decoding knobs from Section 3.4.2. - **Terms met but not taught:** attention, KV cache, instruction tuning, SentencePiece, repetition penalty. - **Open threads:** the $D/N$ exponent question in Section 3.5; whether prediction implies understanding (Section 6).", "u4": "# Session map ::: {.keyidea title=\"Outcome of this session\"} After this session you can: 1. explain a prompt as the *prefix an LLM continues* (Unit 3), and build zero-shot, few-shot, role and chain-of-thought prompts for a task; 2. budget a **context window**, and say what is silently lost when a conversation gets too long; 3. make a model's output **machine-readable**: schema, validation, repair and retry, and know what each can and cannot fix; 4. build a **tool-calling** loop with validated arguments, and say what can go wrong; 5. **evaluate prompts honestly**: derive majority-vote accuracy, run a paired comparison with a confidence interval, and say how many test items you need. ::: - **Prerequisites:** Unit 3 (session 001): next-token prediction, decoding, temperature and the context window. Unit 2 (session 002): the Transformer, in particular that attention cost grows with context length. Basic probability (binomial distribution) for Section 3.3. - **Difficulty ladder used:** easy \u2192 medium \u2192 medium-hard \u2192 hard (Sections 3.1 to 3.4); the hardest tier is posed as open questions in Section 3.5. - **Deliverables:** this document (.tex, .pdf, .docx), 5 figures, the module `codes/llm/prompting_tools/` (templates, context budgeting, JSON validation and repair, tool calling with a mock model, majority-vote and bootstrap statistics; 9 tests), the *Context window budget* and *Majority vote* widgets, and a Colab notebook for the three syllabus projects with a real small model. - **Source anchors:** Brown et al. 2020, arXiv:2005.14165 (few-shot prompting); Wei et al. 2022, arXiv:2201.11903 (chain-of-thought; the quoted result, a PaLM 540B model with eight exemplars reaching state-of-the-art accuracy on GSM8K, is from its introduction); Kojima et al. 2022, arXiv:2205.11916 (the \"Let's think step by step\" zero-shot prompt); Wang et al. 2022, arXiv:2203.11171 (self-consistency, \"sample-and-marginalize\", majority vote in its Table 1); Liu et al. 2023, arXiv:2307.03172 (U-shaped use of long contexts). All IDs and quoted statements were checked in the PDFs on the day of writing. - **An honest limit of this session.** There is no language model and no API key on the machine that built this course. Everything here that is *measured* uses either exact mathematics, a clearly labelled **simulation**, or a **mock model** that only tests the plumbing (it understands nothing). Statements about how real models behave are cited to the papers above and were not re-measured. The practicals with a real model are in a Colab notebook that was **not executed** while writing this document. # 1. Terms: the term tree | Term | Symbol | One-line meaning | Where it appears | New / known | |:-----|:------:|:-----------------|:-----------------|:-----------:| | prompt | | the text (prefix) the model is asked to continue | 3.1 | new | | system / user / assistant / tool message | | the roles in a chat request | 3.1 | new | | zero-shot, few-shot prompting | | task description only / plus worked examples | 3.1 | new | | in-context learning | | the model adapts to examples in the prompt, with no weight update | 3.1 | new | | chain-of-thought (CoT) | | the model writes intermediate steps before the answer | 3.2 | new | | prompt template | | a fixed prompt with placeholders filled in per request | 3.2 | new | | structured output | | output that must follow a schema such as JSON | 3.2 | new | | function / tool calling | | the model asks your code to run a function | 3.2 | new | | context window management | | deciding what text to keep when the history exceeds the limit | 3.2 | new | | self-consistency | | sample several answers and take the majority | 3.3 | new | | paired comparison | | compare two prompts on the same test items | 3.3 | new | | confidence interval | CI | a range of plausible values for the true effect | 3.3 | new | | winner's curse | | significant results overstate the true effect | 3.3 | new | | prompt injection | | text in the input that tries to override your instructions | 3.4 | new | Terms *used but not taught here*: retrieval and chunking (Unit 5), fine-tuning (Unit 6), agents (Unit 8), the full treatment of prompt injection and evaluation (Unit 9). # 2. Remember ::: {.definition title=\"Prompt and chat messages\"} A **prompt** is the text a model is given to continue. Chat APIs structure it as a list of **messages** with roles: **system** (standing instructions), **user** (the request), **assistant** (earlier model replies) and **tool** (results of function calls). The model is *stateless*: on every call you send the whole conversation again. ::: ::: {.definition title=\"Zero-shot, few-shot, chain-of-thought\"} **Zero-shot:** instructions only. **Few-shot:** instructions plus a few input-output examples (Brown et al. 2020); the model adapts from the examples in the prompt, which is called **in-context learning**, with no change to its weights. **Chain-of-thought:** the prompt (examples or the phrase \"Let's think step by step\") makes the model write intermediate reasoning before the final answer (Wei et al. 2022; Kojima et al. 2022). ::: ::: {.definition title=\"Structured output and tool calling\"} **Structured output:** the reply must be, for example, JSON that matches a **schema** (required keys, types, allowed values). **Tool calling:** the model replies with a request such as `{\"tool\": \"calculator\", \"arguments\": {\"expression\": \"23*(4+6)\"}}`; *your code* validates and runs it and sends the result back as a `tool` message; the model then writes the final answer. ::: **Memory hooks.** - *A prompt is a steering wheel, not a program.* You change the *prefix*, and the model continues it with the most plausible text (Unit 3). - *The model has no memory; you re-send it.* Every turn costs the whole history again. - *Never trust, always validate.* Model output is *input* to your code; check it like user input. **Recall quiz** (answers in Section 10). 1. What do the four message roles do? 2. Why does a chat that is 7 turns long cost far more than 7 times one turn? 3. Name two things `parse_json_lenient` can repair and two it cannot. 4. If one sample is right with probability $0.4$, does a majority vote of 21 samples help? 5. Why is a 5-point accuracy gain on 100 test items usually not convincing? # 3. Understand: the derivation ## 3.1 Tier 1 (easy): intuition and a picture Unit 3 showed that an LLM does one thing: given a prefix, it gives a probability to every possible next token. **Prompt engineering is the craft of choosing the prefix.** A request such as \"Translate to French: cheese\" works because, in the model's training text, that prefix is usually followed by a French word. Four moves cover most of the craft. 1. **Say what you want, clearly** (task, format, audience, constraints). This is zero-shot prompting. 2. **Show what you want.** Add two or three worked examples (few-shot). The model copies their pattern: format, tone, labels. 3. **Give a role and rules** in the system message (\"You are a support agent for ... Answer only from the provided text\"). 4. **Let it think on paper.** Ask for step-by-step reasoning before the answer. All of this text must fit in the **context window**, together with the room needed for the reply. Figure 1 packs a 23-message conversation into two window sizes. ![A conversation drawn to scale (purple = system prompt, blue = user messages, green = assistant messages; the long blue block is retrieved documents). Top: a 4096-token window holds everything (3220 tokens plus a 500-token reserve for the reply). Middle: with a 2048-token window the oldest messages are dropped first: all three few-shot examples and the retrieved documents disappear (hatched), exactly the parts the model needs. Bottom: pinning the three examples keeps them, but the retrieved documents (1500 tokens) still cannot fit.](figures/fig01_budget.png){width=95%} Read Figure 1 as a warning. A simple \"keep the newest messages\" rule is *silent*: nothing tells the model, or you, that its instructions' examples and the evidence for the answer are gone. The module's `fit_context` therefore has an explicit `pin` flag for messages that must stay. ## 3.2 Tier 2 (medium): the definitions made precise ### 3.2.1 Templates A **prompt template** is a string with placeholders that is filled for each request. The most common failure is a silent typo (a placeholder left unfilled, or a variable that never appears). The module's `render` is deliberately strict: ```python render(\"Summarise {{text}} in {{n}} bullet points\", text=doc, n=3) # fine render(\"Summarise {{text}} in {{n}} bullet points\", text=doc) # KeyError: missing template variables ['n'] render(\"Summarise {{text}}\", text=doc, style=\"formal\") # ValueError: unused variables ['style'] ``` A **few-shot prompt** is a template whose body is a list of messages: the system message, then each example as a (user, assistant) pair, then the real query (`few_shot_messages`). ### 3.2.2 Conversation cost: the model has no memory Each call re-sends the system prompt (of $s$ tokens) and the whole history. If every turn adds $t$ tokens to the history (user message plus reply), over $K$ turns the total input processed is $$ \\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\big(s+k\\,t\\big)=K\\,s+t\\,\\frac{K(K+1)}{2}. $$ {#eq:convcost} (this approximates the input at turn $k$ as $s+k\\,t$: the $k-1$ earlier turns plus the new message counted as a full turn). It grows **quadratically** in the number of turns. Worked check with $s=150$, $t=170$ (50 for the user, 120 for the reply), $K=7$: $7\\cdot150+170\\cdot28=5810$ tokens processed in total, against $150+170\\cdot7=1340$ tokens if the model remembered by itself. This is why long chats get expensive and why history must be trimmed or summarised. ### 3.2.3 Chain-of-thought Wei et al. (2022) show that giving the model examples whose answers *include their reasoning steps* improves its performance on arithmetic, commonsense and symbolic tasks, and that this ability \"emerges\" in sufficiently large models. Their headline: a PaLM 540B model prompted with just eight chain-of-thought exemplars reached state-of-the-art accuracy on the GSM8K benchmark of math word problems. Kojima et al. (2022) found that even with *no* examples, adding \"Let's think step by step\" elicits reasoning. **An intuition for why it can help (not a theorem).** A Transformer with $L$ layers performs a bounded amount of sequential computation to produce each token. A problem that needs many sequential steps cannot be solved \"in the head\" in one token. If the model *writes* the intermediate results, each written token becomes part of the context for the next, which gives it more sequential steps. I state this as a heuristic; why chain-of-thought works is an open research topic (Section 3.5), and Wei et al. report gains only for large models. ### 3.2.4 Structured output: validate, repair, retry If your code must read the model's reply, ask for JSON and **treat the reply as untrusted input**. Three layers: 1. **Validate** against a schema. The module's `validate` supports `type`, `required`, `properties`, `enum`, `minimum`, `maximum` and `items`. Example: `{\"label\": \"mixed\"}` fails with `$.label: 'mixed' not in ['positive', 'negative', 'neutral']`. 2. **Repair** harmless formatting damage with `parse_json_lenient`: strip a code fence, cut prose around the JSON, remove a trailing comma, turn single quotes into double quotes. 3. **Retry** with feedback (`structured_call`): append the validation errors as a new user message and ask again. Figure 2 measures layer 2 on 300 synthetic outputs per damage type, made by corrupting valid JSON (these are damage patterns I applied; how often real models produce each pattern is not measured here). ![Left: share of 300 corrupted outputs that `parse_json_lenient` can parse (light blue) and that then also pass the schema (dark blue). Code fences, surrounding prose, trailing commas and single quotes are repaired every time. Truncated JSON and unquoted keys are never repaired (0%). A syntactically valid but wrong value (a label outside the allowed set) parses (100%) but fails the schema (0%), so validation is a separate step. Right: probability of a valid answer within $r+1$ attempts, $1-(1-q)^{r+1}$, if each attempt is valid with probability $q$ independently.](figures/fig04_repair.png){width=95%} The right-hand curve shows why retries are cheap *if attempts are independent*: with $q=0.5$ per attempt, three retries give $1-0.5^4=0.94$. When the model fails for a systematic reason (the schema is ambiguous), attempts are not independent and the curve is too optimistic. ### 3.2.5 Tool calling The model cannot run code or read your database; it can only *write text*. Tool calling turns text into action under your control: 1. you describe each tool to the model (a name, a description, a JSON schema for its arguments); 2. the model replies with a tool request in a fixed format; 3. **your code** checks the arguments against the schema, runs the function, and catches any error; 4. the result goes back as a `tool` message and the model writes the final answer. ![The message flow of one tool call, drawn from the real log of the module's `run_tool_loop` with a mock model: the user's request, the model's JSON tool request, the tool result 230 computed by your code, and the final answer.](figures/fig05_toolloop.png){width=75%} The module's `ToolRegistry.call` returns errors as *text* (`error: unknown tool 'x'`, `error: $: missing required key 'expression'`) so a bad request does not crash the loop and the model can correct itself. The calculator tool is a lesson in itself: while writing it I first allowed any string made of digits and the operators, which still accepts `9**9**9`, a computation that never finishes. The final `safe_calc` rejects `**` and long inputs, and a whitelist plus `eval` is still only a teaching shortcut; production code should use a real expression parser. ## 3.3 Tier 3 (medium-hard): the main derivations ### 3.3.1 Self-consistency: when does voting help? Wang et al. (2022) sample several reasoning paths at a non-zero temperature (Unit 3) and take the **majority answer**; their Table 1 compares aggregation rules on PaLM 540B. Under a simple model we can compute exactly when this helps. Suppose each sample is correct with probability $p$, independently, and wrong answers are spread over many different values, so they do not combine into a competing majority. With an odd number $k$ of samples the majority is correct when more than $k/2$ are correct: $$ A_k(p)=\\sum_{j=\\lceil (k+1)/2\\rceil}^{k}\\binom{k}{j}p^{j}(1-p)^{k-j}. $$ {#eq:major} For $k=3$: $A_3(p)=p^3+3p^2(1-p)=3p^2-2p^3$, and $$ \\begin{aligned} A_3(p)-p&=3p^2-2p^3-p=-p\\,(2p^2-3p+1)\\\\ &=-p\\,(2p-1)(p-1)=p\\,(1-p)\\,(2p-1). &&\\text{(factor the quadratic)} \\end{aligned} $$ {#eq:major3} Since $p(1-p)>0$ for $0<p<1$, voting helps exactly when $2p-1>0$, that is **$p>\\tfrac12$**; it hurts when $p<\\tfrac12$ and does nothing at $p=\\tfrac12$. Worked check: $p=0.7$ gives $A_3=3(0.49)-2(0.343)=0.784>0.7$. The same holds for every odd $k$, because $A_k(p)+A_k(1-p)=1$ (the majority is correct or wrong, and swapping $p\\leftrightarrow1-p$ swaps the two). ![Accuracy of the majority answer against the number of samples $k$, for several single-sample accuracies $p$ (exact, from @eq:major), with a simulation of 200{,}000 trials for $p=0.6$ (black dots). At $p=0.6$: 0.683 at $k=5$, 0.753 at $k=11$, 0.826 at $k=21$, 0.872 at $k=31$; the simulation gives 0.601, 0.682, 0.754, 0.825, 0.872. At $p=0.5$ nothing changes; at $p=0.4$ voting makes it worse (0.317, 0.247, 0.174, 0.128).](figures/fig02_majority.png){width=75%} **What the assumption hides.** Real samples from one model are *not* independent: if the model has a misconception, it repeats it, and the wrong answers can agree. Then the true majority accuracy is lower than @eq:major, and the gain from adding samples stops early. Equation @eq:major is an upper-bound intuition, not a forecast. ### 3.3.2 Comparing two prompts: paired data and sample size You have two prompts A and B and a test set of $n$ items. Score each item 1 (correct) or 0. Because both prompts see the *same* items, compare them item by item. Let $d_i=\\text{score}_B(i)-\\text{score}_A(i)\\in\\{-1,0,+1\\}$. Let $b$ be the count of items where only B is right and $c$ where only A is right. Then $$ \\bar d=\\frac{b-c}{n},\\qquad \\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}(d_i)=\\frac{b+c}{n}-\\bar d^{\\,2},\\qquad \\mathrm{SE}(\\bar d)=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}(d_i)}{n}}, $$ {#eq:paired} (the first term of the variance is the share of items where the prompts disagree, since $d_i^2=1$ exactly there), and an approximate 95% confidence interval is $\\bar d\\pm1.96\\,\\mathrm{SE}$. **Worked example:** $n=100$, $b=15$, $c=8$. Then $\\bar d=0.07$, $\\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}=0.23-0.0049=0.2251$, $\\mathrm{SE}=\\sqrt{0.2251/100}=0.0474$, and the interval is $0.07\\pm0.093=[-0.023,\\ 0.163]$. It contains 0: **a 7-point gain on 100 items is not distinguishable from noise.** How many items are needed? To detect a true gain $\\delta$ with 80% power at the 5% level, the standard approximation is $$ n\\;\\approx\\;\\frac{(1.96+0.84)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{Var}(d_i)}{\\delta^{2}}. $$ {#eq:npower} In the simulation below the two prompts disagree on 15.5% of the items (variance $0.152$) and the true gain is $\\delta=0.05$, so $n\\approx7.84\\cdot0.152/0.0025=478$. The **bootstrap** gives the same kind of interval without formulas: resample the $n$ values $d_i$ with replacement many times and take the 2.5% and 97.5% quantiles of the resampled means (`paired_bootstrap_ci`). ![A simulation (not real prompts): outcomes for two prompts with true accuracies 0.70 and 0.75, which agree on most items. Left: the share of experiments in which the 95% bootstrap interval excludes zero, against the number of test items (150 simulated experiments per point). With a real gain the share is 9% at $n=25$, 23% at $n=100$, 47% at $n=200$, 75% at $n=400$, 94% at $n=800$. With no real gain it stays near 5% (0.03 to 0.06), as it should. Right: six simulated test sets of 100 items: two of the six intervals include zero (grey).](figures/fig03_power.png){width=95%} Figure 5 agrees with @eq:npower: 80% power needs roughly 450 to 500 items for a 5-point gain. ### 3.3.3 The winner's curse If you run many small experiments and report only the significant ones, the reported gains are inflated. In the same simulation at $n=100$ the average measured gain over all test sets is $0.0501$ (true gain $0.05$, standard deviation $0.039$): the estimator is unbiased. But only 22.5% of the experiments were significant, and among *those* the average measured gain was $0.100$, **twice the true effect**. Selecting on significance selects lucky samples. The practical rule: fix the test set and the metric *before* trying prompt variants, and be sceptical of a large gain found on a small set. ## 3.4 Tier 4 (hard): robustness and risk ### 3.4.1 Long contexts Attention cost grows with context length (session 002), but there is a second issue: models do not use all positions equally. Liu et al. (2023) found a **U-shaped** curve: models use relevant information best at the very beginning or end of the input, and performance degrades when it sits in the middle, even for models built for long contexts. A practical consequence for Figure 1: put the instructions and the question at the edges, and do not assume that more retrieved text is always better. ### 3.4.2 Constrained output Instead of repairing bad JSON afterwards, a serving system can prevent it: at every step, set the logit of every token that would break the grammar or schema to $-\\infty$ *before* the softmax of Unit 3, $$ p_i=\\frac{e^{z_i/T}\\,\\mathbf{1}[i\\text{ allowed}]}{\\sum_j e^{z_j/T}\\,\\mathbf{1}[j\\text{ allowed}]}, $$ the same mechanism as top-$k$ filtering, with the allowed set decided by the schema instead of by rank. This guarantees *syntax*; it cannot guarantee that the *content* is true. ### 3.4.3 Prompt injection (preview) Your prompt contains text you control (the system message) and text you do not (the user's message, a web page, a retrieved document). A model cannot reliably tell the two apart: a retrieved page that says \"ignore previous instructions and ...\" is just more text in the prefix. Practical defences (Unit 9 treats them properly): keep secrets and permissions out of the prompt, validate every tool call as in Section 3.2.5, give tools the least privilege, and never run model output as code. ## 3.5 Tier 5 (hardest): open questions 1. **Why does chain-of-thought work, and are the written steps the model's real reasoning?** The text of a chain of thought may be a plausible story rather than a record of the computation. Whether the steps are *faithful* to what the model actually did is an active research question; do not use a model's explanation as evidence that its answer is right. 2. **Prompt sensitivity.** Small changes in wording, example order or formatting can change measured accuracy. How to report a result so it survives such changes is unsettled; the minimum is to test several prompt variants on a fixed, large test set (Section 3.3.2). 3. **When is a prompt better than fine-tuning?** There is no general rule. Units 5 and 6 give the trade-offs (retrieval, fine-tuning). # 4. Apply ## 4.1 Worked problems **Problem 1 (template).** `render(\"Hi {{name}}\", nam=\"Ann\")` raises: the variable is called `name`, not `nam`. Silent failure is avoided because both the missing and the unused name are reported. **Problem 2 (context budget).** A window of 4096 tokens, a reserved reply of 500, a system prompt of 150, three examples of 100 tokens each and a new question of 80. Free room for history and retrieved text: $4096-500-150-300-80=3066$ tokens. **Problem 3 (conversation cost).** A bot with a 150-token system prompt, 170 tokens per turn, 7 turns: 5810 input tokens in total by @eq:convcost, against 1340 if only new text counted, a factor of 4.3. **Problem 4 (voting).** $p=0.7$, $k=3$: $A_3=0.784$. With $k=5$: $\\sum_{j=3}^{5}\\binom5jp^j(1-p)^{5-j}=0.837$ (the table of Figure 4 gives 0.837). **Problem 5 (is B better?).** $n=100$, $b=15$, $c=8$: the 95% interval is $[-0.023,0.163]$, so no. If the same proportions held with $n=400$ ($b=60$, $c=32$): $\\bar d=0.07$, $\\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}=0.23-0.0049=0.2251$, $\\mathrm{SE}=\\sqrt{0.2251/400}=0.0237$, interval $[0.024,0.116]$: now it excludes zero. ## 4.2 Code and figures Everything is in `codes/llm/prompting_tools/` (see its README). The three syllabus projects fit the same skeleton, with the model hidden behind one method so you can swap the mock for a real one: ```python class MyLLM: # any object with complete(messages) -> str def complete(self, messages): ... # call your provider here # 1. domain chatbot: system prompt + few-shot examples + fit_context to keep the history in bounds msgs = P.few_shot_messages(system, examples, user_question) kept, dropped = P.fit_context(msgs, window=4096, reserve_output=500) # 2. document summariser: template + structured output obj, attempts = P.structured_call(llm, msgs, {\"type\": \"object\", \"required\": [\"summary\", \"bullets\"], ...}) # 3. assistant with tools: registry + validated loop answer, log = P.run_tool_loop(llm, registry, \"Please calculate 23 * (4 + 6)\") ``` With the bundled `MockLLM` the loop returns \"The answer is 230.\" and the log of Figure 5. The mock only tests the plumbing: it pattern-matches arithmetic and understands nothing. The Colab notebook `u4_prompting_real_model.ipynb` replaces it with a small instruction-tuned model from the Hugging Face hub (it was **not executed** while writing this document). ::: {.keyidea title=\"Try it in the playground\"} The **Context window budget** widget lets you set the window and the size of each part of a conversation and see what is dropped and what the next turn costs. The **Majority vote** widget plots the accuracy of voting for any single-sample accuracy $p$ and number of samples $k$, next to a simulation. ::: # 5. Analyze *Checklist items used: limits (5.1), dominance (5.2), sensitivity (5.3), failure modes (5.4), constructed-versus-measured (5.5).* ## 5.1 Limits - $k=1$: $A_1(p)=p$. $p=\\tfrac12$: $A_k=\\tfrac12$ for every odd $k$ (Figure 4). $p\\to1$: $A_k\\to1$ quickly ($p=0.8$, $k=11$ gives 0.988). - Conversation of one turn: @eq:convcost reduces to $s+t$. - A pinned message that is too large: `fit_context` raises `ValueError` instead of silently truncating. ## 5.2 Which term dominates In @eq:convcost the history term $t\\,K(K+1)/2$ overtakes the system-prompt term $K\\,s$ once $t(K+1)/2>s$; with $s=150$ and $t=170$ that is already true from the first turn ($K=1$: $170>150$). So for chats, history, not the system prompt, dominates the cost. ## 5.3 Sensitivity Majority accuracy is most sensitive to $p$ near $\\tfrac12$: at $k=21$, $A_k(0.55)=0.679$ but $A_k(0.6)=0.826$. A small improvement in single-sample accuracy is amplified when voting. Evaluation power is most sensitive to the disagreement rate: if two prompts disagree on only 5% of the items, $\\mathrm{Var}(d_i)\\approx0.05$ and @eq:npower asks for about 157 items; at 30% disagreement it asks for 941. ## 5.4 Failure modes - Silently dropped instructions or evidence (Figure 1). - Syntax repaired but content wrong (Figure 2, last row). - Tool requests that are valid JSON but dangerous (`9**9**9`). - Voting with dependent samples (Section 3.3.1). - Declaring a winner from a small set (Section 3.3.2), or from the significant subset only (Section 3.3.3). ## 5.5 Constructed versus measured Figures 2 and 5 are **constructed**: the damage patterns and the prompt outcomes were generated by code, to show the mathematics. Figure 4 is exact mathematics (with a simulation check). Figures 1 and 3 use made-up message sizes and a mock model. Nothing in this session measures a real model, and the claims about real models (chain-of-thought gains, the U-shaped context curve) are cited, not reproduced. # 6. Evaluate **Prompting versus the alternatives.** Prompting needs no training and is easy to change, but is limited by the context window and by what the model already knows. Retrieval (Unit 5) adds knowledge; fine-tuning (Unit 6) changes behaviour permanently. Prompting is the right first thing to try; it is the wrong tool when the knowledge changes daily (use retrieval) or when you need a consistent style across millions of calls (consider fine-tuning). **Chain-of-thought and voting: cost.** Both multiply the tokens you pay for: chain-of-thought lengthens each answer; voting over $k$ samples costs $k$ times as much. Figure 4 shows what the extra cost buys *under independence*; measure on your task. **Structured output.** Constrained decoding is the strongest guarantee of syntax; validation with retry is the portable fallback that works with any provider. **When not to trust a prompt result.** A gain on fewer than a few hundred items; a result found by trying many variants on one test set; a prompt whose success depends on one lucky example order. **Wider context.** Prompt engineering is partly empirical craft and partly software engineering: versioned templates, tests, validation and logs. The durable skills are the ones in this session (budgeting context, validating outputs, measuring honestly); specific phrasings come and go with each model release. # 7. Create ::: {.create title=\"What you can build from this\"} 1. **Domain chatbot:** a system prompt, three few-shot examples and `fit_context` with pinned examples; show with a long fake conversation that the examples survive. 2. **Document summariser:** a template, a JSON schema (`summary`, `bullets`, `confidence`), and `structured_call`; list the failure cases your validator catches. 3. **Tool-using assistant:** add a second tool (a date function or a lookup in a small dictionary) and make the registry reject calls with wrong types. 4. **Prompt A/B harness:** a function that takes two prompts, a test set and a model, runs both, and prints the paired difference with a bootstrap interval and the number of items still needed. 5. **Combine with Unit 3:** use the sampler from session 001 to generate $k$ samples at a temperature, then take the majority answer; plot accuracy against $k$. 6. **Module for the open-source package:** add a JSON-schema `oneOf` and `pattern` check to `validate`, with tests. ::: # 8. Visualization lesson | Chart type | Best for | How it misleads | matplotlib call | |:--|:--|:--|:--| | to-scale stacked bar (a \"budget bar\") | parts of a limited resource (Fig. 1) | looks exact when token counts are only estimates | `ax.barh(0, w, left=x)` in a loop | | line plot with simulation overlay | checking a formula against a simulation (Fig. 4) | few simulation points hide noise; show the trial count | `ax.plot(k, exact); ax.plot(kk, mc, \"ko\")` | | interval (error-bar) plot | an estimate with uncertainty (Fig. 5 right) | the interval width depends on the method (bootstrap, normal) | `ax.errorbar(d, i, xerr=[[d-lo],[hi-d]], fmt=\"o\")` | | power curve | sample size planning (Fig. 5 left) | assumes the simulated effect and disagreement rate | `ax.semilogx(n, share, \"o-\")` | | message-flow diagram | who sends what to whom (Fig. 3) | looks like a log but is hand-laid | `ax.annotate(\"\", xy, xytext, arrowprops=...)` | Table: charts taught here. How to read a power curve: find the point where it crosses 0.8 on the y-axis and read the number of items on the x-axis; the second curve shows the false-positive rate, which should sit at 0.05. # 9. Problem set ::: {.exercise title=\"Graded exercises\"} 1. (easy) Write a few-shot prompt (three examples) that classifies a review as positive or negative, and say which of the four message roles each part belongs to. 2. (medium) A window of 8192 tokens, a reserve of 1000 for the reply and a system prompt of 400. Each turn adds 250 tokens. How many full turns fit before the oldest must be dropped? 3. (medium-hard) Prove that $A_k(p)+A_k(1-p)=1$ for odd $k$ and use it to show $A_k(\\tfrac12)=\\tfrac12$. 4. (hard) Two prompts have $n=250$, $b=30$, $c=14$. Compute $\\bar d$, $\\mathrm{SE}$ and the 95% interval with @eq:paired. Does it exclude zero? What $n$ would a 80%-power test of a true gain of $0.03$ need if the disagreement rate is 0.18? 5. (hardest) In Section 3.3.3 the average measured gain among significant experiments was $0.100$ for a true gain of $0.05$. Explain why this happens, then explain why it would not happen if you reported *all* experiments, and say what this implies for a team that tries 20 prompts on the same 100 test items and reports the best. ::: # 10. Retrieval practice and recap **Answers to the Section 2 quiz.** 1. System: standing rules. User: the request. Assistant: the model's earlier replies. Tool: results of functions your code ran. 2. The model is stateless, so each turn re-sends the whole history: the cost grows like $K(K+1)/2$ (Equation @eq:convcost), not like $K$. 3. Repairs: code fences, surrounding prose, trailing commas, single quotes. Cannot repair: truncated JSON, unquoted keys; and it cannot fix a wrong *value* that is valid JSON (Figure 2). 4. No: with $p=0.4<\\tfrac12$ the majority gets worse (0.174 at $k=21$). 5. With 100 items the 95% interval for a 5-point gain is roughly $\\pm0.09$ wide, so it includes zero; about 450 to 500 items are needed (Figure 5). **Explain it to a friend.** - Why does the same chatbot cost more for the 10th question than for the 1st? - Why must code treat a model's JSON like untrusted user input? - Why can \"7 points better on 100 questions\" still be noise? **Re-derive tomorrow without looking:** (a) $A_3(p)-p=p(1-p)(2p-1)$; (b) the cost @eq:convcost; (c) the paired interval @eq:paired for $n=100$, $b=15$, $c=8$. # 11. Where next - **Next node:** Unit 5 (retrieval-augmented generation): the retrieved documents of Figure 1 are what RAG supplies, and the evaluation tools of Section 3.3 are how you will judge it. - **Terms met but not taught:** constrained decoding grammars, prompt caching, LLM-as-judge (Unit 9). - **Open threads:** whether written reasoning steps are faithful (Section 3.5); how to report prompt results so they survive wording changes."};
